894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278483 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186105894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186105894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278492 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186105895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278484 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186105895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186105895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186105910 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278387 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186105910 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278389 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186105912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278386 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102222 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186106061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277935 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106061 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k,\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106061 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n$f(x) = {\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + k,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186106061 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}+kx]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{8}{12}+2k]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}+2k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102258 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186106067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277900 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106067 1071 "![](images/36/365c6e6ebc861b9d8276773d5afe39b971096f0de6f07fced2c5a2ea9e035554.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n$∬_(S)3xdS$,\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z = x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186106067 1071 "![](images/36/365c6e6ebc861b9d8276773d5afe39b971096f0de6f07fced2c5a2ea9e035554.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106067 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{r}^{3}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102276 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186106070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278087 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106070 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n$∬_(S)(z)/(\\sqrt{1 + 2\\cos^{2} (x + y)})dS$\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z = \\sin (x + y)$ for $0 \\le x \\le π$ and $-x \\le y \\le x$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186106070 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ and $-x\\le y\\le x.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186106070 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y))^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278273 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278274 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278275 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278276 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278282 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278283 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278284 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278285 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186115311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278277 131941395