in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2x$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134129 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130912 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186134131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277770 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134131 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x = y^{2} + z^{2}$ and the plane $x = 2y$. Given its representation\n$R = {(x, y, z): a \\le y \\le b, g_{1}(y) \\le z \\le g_{2}(y), f_{1}(y, z) \\le x \\le f_{2}(y, z)}$\nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{1}(y, z) = y^{2} + z^{2}$ and $f_{2}(y, z) = 2y$\n2. $g_{1}(y) = 0$ and $g_{2}(y) = 1 - y^{2}$\n3. $a =-1$ and $b = 1$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186134131 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2y$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=0$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=1-{y}^{2}$\n- $a=-1$ and $b=1$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134131 1074 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $x=2y,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{2y}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/01/01cb7f25e98c8607258f673a5c5be6a1e55869b379c4484c05a04cebbe5dc5f6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130924 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186134133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278127 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134133 1071 "![](images/c0/c0745df4ba2b63cb0479743f03df384c1fd70d74851b9feaecdb88b62618220e.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)12xzdV$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186134133 1071 "![](images/c0/c0745df4ba2b63cb0479743f03df384c1fd70d74851b9feaecdb88b62618220e.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}12xz\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186134133 1074 "![](images/c1/c1853c772e219f53a0b394bd0b50beea6b33c451afc453983cdccb1df333c5ea.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0620f8f25174cb71528049341669458c68a7ccfdd3ff76b5f62c878307080419.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x-z}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}12xz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12xz[y]_{y=0}^{y=4-x-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12xz(4-x-z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[24x{z}^{2}-6{x}^{2}{z}^{2}-4x{z}^{3}]_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(64x-24{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(64x-24{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[32{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(32-8)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186152194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278189 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278323 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278324 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278325 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278332 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278333 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278334 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157304 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278385 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278390 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278391 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278394 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157306 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278380 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153928 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186157619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277988 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157619 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u(1+v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=y(t),\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157619 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = u - v, y = u(1 + v)$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n$x =-t, y = y (t), 1 \\ge t \\ge 0$.\n\nFind the function $y(t)$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186157619 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(1+v)) \\\\  & =(0-v,\\,0(1+v)) \\\\  & =(-v,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-v,\\,y=0,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=0,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153946 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186157622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277909 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157622 1071 "![](images/b0/b0e8a196dc27f333db4179b5c318ccd3aa4b385c0752c570fbfedc0226b3b15b.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $2$ and $3,$ as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186157622 1071 "![](images/b0/b0e8a196dc27f333db4179b5c318ccd3aa4b385c0752c570fbfedc0226b3b15b.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $∬_(D)(1)/((x^{2} + y^{2})^{2})dA$, where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $2$ and $3$, as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186157622 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{4}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{2{r}^{2}}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{18})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278201 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278300 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278301 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278305 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278307 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278426 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278427 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278432 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278420 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278422 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278425 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278423 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278424 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278202 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278203 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278204 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278205 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186166897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278206 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186167135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163858 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186167135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277968 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186167135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186167135 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x, y, 2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z = 2x + 3y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le y \\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n$.\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $\\mid \\mid r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} \\mid |= \\sqrt{14}$.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186167135 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+3y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{14}.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186167135 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+3y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+3y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨x,\\,y,\\,4x+6y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(-2x-3y+4x+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}}(2x+3y)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{14}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+3xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186172062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278359 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186172062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278363 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186172136 10