38 #edb/ref 17592186278250 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278251 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278252 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278253 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278254 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278255 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278257 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278308 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278309 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278310 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278311 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278574 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186192808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278576 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191038 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186193197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277982 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193197 1071 "For the function $f(x, y) = ye^{xy} - x^{2}y^{2}$, evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1, 1)$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186193197 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,1).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193197 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product, the chain, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot {e}^{xy}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}))-{x}^{2}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy))-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =({e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =({e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot 1)-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}-2{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,1)} & ={e}^{1\\cdot 1}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 1}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =e+e-2 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191128 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186193212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278083 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193212 1071 "Consider the set $A$, defined by\n$A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: 4x^{2} + y^{2} \\ge 4}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $intA∩∂A = ∅$\n2. $A - ∂A = intA$\n3. $∂A⊂A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186193212 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193212 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c410c21e79e1bc688c1ff1a3440a207c62bcad42525bdc93fa2770b76d48bd.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33a4738b642443c2535d347aa53ee2b7b80c800142c4844d94578769118ebfbc.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191134 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186193213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277756 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193213 1071 "Consider the set $A$, defined by\n$A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: y < 1 - x}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n2. $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n3. $intA = A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186193213 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<1-x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186193213 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5151df3c5b84ffaca9aa16b401cd9364fea10a240d39c9c28d815b36e417b16d.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278312 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278352 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278353 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278356 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278318 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278319 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278320 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278321 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278322 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278354 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278388 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278393 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278349 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278350 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278351 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278452 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278476 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278477 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198384 1038 #edb/ref 17592