5487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198583 1071 "![](images/1f/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198583 1071 "![](images/1f/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{5} \\le v \\le \\frac{π}{3}}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198583 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{2},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/61/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194117 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278036 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198586 1071 "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198586 1071 "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{6} \\le v \\le \\frac{π}{3}}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n1. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{π}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n2. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{π}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n3. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198586 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194147 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278167 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198591 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u, v)$, where\n\n$F(x, y, z) = ⟨2x + y, 2z, y + z⟩, r(u, v) = ⟨ - u^{2}, 2u^{2} - v^{2}, v^{2}⟩, (u, v) ∈ D⊂R^{2}$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)AAAAAAAdA$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} = ⟨8uv, 4uv, 4uv⟩$.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198591 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2x+y,\\,2z,\\,y+z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨-{u}^{2},\\,2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨8uv,\\,4uv,\\,4uv⟩.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198591 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨8uv,\\,4uv,\\,4uv⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2x+y,\\,2z,\\,y+z⟩{|}_{x=-{u}^{2}\\,y=2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2(-{u}^{2})+(2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}),\\,2({v}^{2}),\\,(2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2})+{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{v}^{2},\\,2{v}^{2},\\,2{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-{v}^{2},\\,2{v}^{2},\\,2{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨8uv,\\,4uv,\\,4uv⟩ \\\\  & =(-{v}^{2})\\cdot 8uv+2{v}^{2}\\cdot 4uv+2{u}^{2}\\cdot 4uv \\\\  & =-8u{v}^{3}+8u{v}^{3}+8{u}^{3}v \\\\  & =8{u}^{3}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,8{u}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194153 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277775 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198592 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3x+y+z,\\,\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\,-3x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-1,\\,2v,\\,2-3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 3.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198592 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$∬_(S)⟨3x + y + z, \\frac{y}{2} - 2,-3x - z⟩ \\cdot dS$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$r(u, v) = ⟨u - 1, 2v, 2 - 3u - 2v⟩$\n\nfor $-1 \\le u \\le 1$ and $0 \\le v \\le 3$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198592 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3x+y+z,\\,\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\,-3x-z⟩|}_{x=u-1\\,y=2v\\,z=2-3u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨3(u-1)+(2v)+(2-3u-2v),\\,\\frac{2v}{2}-2,\\,-3(u-1)-(2-3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,v-2,\\,2v+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-1,\\,v-2,\\,2v+1⟩\\cdot ⟨6,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6+(v-2)\\cdot 2+(2v+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6+2v-4+4v+2 \\\\  & =6v-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3x+y+z,\\,\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\,-3x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6v-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6v-8)\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}(6v-8)\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[u]_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [3{v}^{2}-8v]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194189 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277833 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198598 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨2x,\\,\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-1,\\,uv,\\,3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨3v,\\,-6,\\,2u⟩.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198598 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$∬_(S)⟨2x, \\frac{xz}{3} - 2y, 2z⟩ \\cdot dS$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$r(u, v) = ⟨2u - 1, uv, 3v⟩$\n\nover the domain $D = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1,-1 \\le v \\le 2}$.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} = ⟨3v,-6, 2u⟩$.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198598 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨3v,\\,-6,\\,2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨2x,\\,\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\,2z⟩|}_{x=2u-1\\,y=uv\\,z=3v} \\\\  & =⟨2(2u-1),\\,\\frac{(2u-1)(3v)}{3}-2uv,\\,2(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4u-2,\\,-v,\\,6v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨4u-2,\\,-v,\\,6v⟩\\cdot ⟨3v,\\,-6,\\,2u⟩ \\\\  & =(4u-2)\\cdot 3v+(-v)\\cdot (-6)+6v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =12uv-6v+6v+12uv \\\\  & =24uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2x,\\,\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\,2z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}24uv\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[12{u}^{2}v]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}12v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[6{v}^{2}]_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =6({2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194201 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277960 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198600 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198600 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$∬_(S)⟨z + y, 2y + z, xy⟩ \\cdot dS$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z = x - y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le y \\le 2$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198600 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\,2y+(x-y),\\,xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{xy}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194237 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277942 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198606 1071 "![](images/9a/9ad934c88b753dd418e66028bebc341e5d2115dc239ed66b9743d028656d1b96.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T$, given by\n\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\n\nwhere\n\n$x = u\\cos v, y = u\\sin v$,\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D$. Which of the following could be the region $Δ$?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198606 1071 "![](images/9a/9ad934c88b753dd418e66028bebc341e5d2115dc239ed66b9743d028656d1b96.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198606 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c5f652a4324d6d2ee99d5dcf5d5f3d7be69fd794ebfcd8b6b0a9300d39dca20.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194243 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277802 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198607 1071 "![](images/89/8901e7155a476f0019b6d66e3b62b579b91daf99a448e497c93efce1d94415b0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨0, 0, 2x + yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198607 1071 "![](images/89/8901e7155a476f0019b6d66e3b62b579b91daf99a448e497c93efce1d94415b0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198607 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/8f/8fa71e16d84eb92506e9b10269182f907cb4f2437d0a9bdd3822da48c4cbba4a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+2xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194249 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277907 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198608 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x, y)$ for $(x, y) ∈ D$, the flux of the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨P, Q, R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)(A\\frac{A}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{A} + R)dA$.\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198608 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\frac{}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198608 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194261 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278063 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198610 1071 "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨0, 0, z(x^{2} + y^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198610 1071 "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198610 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/c7/c7b22b03b9952bfe068e9252f7336568aa8d761c32a1bc31c444ebb97ee925ba.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194267 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277785 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198611 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x, y)$ for $(x, y) ∈ D$, the flux of the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨P, Q, R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)(A\\frac{∂g}{∂x} + A\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA$.\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198611 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198611 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+(-Q\\,)\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194273 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278098 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198612 1071 "![](images/ea/ea320b4b8a036bdf905a51041eb94ffb2464125af912ec74830d49af922f4342.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x, y + 2z, z - 2y⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198612 1071 "![](images/ea/ea320b4b8a036bdf905a51041eb94ffb2464125af912ec74830d49af922f4342.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198612 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+y(y+2z)+z(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz+{z}^{2}-2yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =12\\pi \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194285 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277794 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198614 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198614 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4}\\cos zdxdydz$?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198614 1074 "Since the domain of integration $R=[1,4]\\times [0,2]\\times [0,\\pi /2]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dz & =(4-1)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194291 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277767 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198615 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{z+1}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198615 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{-y}^{0}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}2e^{z + 1}dzdxdy$?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198615 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{z+1}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{z+1}\\,dz]dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2[{e}^{z+1}]_{z=0}^{z=x}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2({e}^{x+1}-e)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2({e}^{x+1}-e)\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{e}^{x+1}-e\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2[{e}^{x+1}-ex]_{x=-y}^{x=0}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{0+1}-e(0)-({e}^{-y+1}-e(-y))dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}e-{e}^{1-y}-ey\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}e-{e}^{1-y}-ey\\,dy & =2{[ey+{e}^{1-y}-\\frac{e}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2(e(1-0)+{e}^{1-1}-{e}^{1-0}-\\frac{e}{2}({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})) \\\\  & =2(e+1-e-\\frac{e}{2}) \\\\  & =2-e\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194303 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278130 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198617 1071 "![](images/04/043adf0ef683c24fbf4680145411d86fa94b52016bee44ac8f08ca7c5ca9f1b7.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)24xydV$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198617 1071 "![](images/04/043adf0ef683c24fbf4680145411d86fa94b52016bee44ac8f08ca7c5ca9f1b7.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}24xy\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198617 1074 "![](images/75/75a76a2dbc34c889335e66623e12b686eb9a255ee0d67f9be0e3792f2650360c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dfa0a80bc323401f050d1302dc42c92558d8b566d9b0f985e955d34eb5cc15bd.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}24xy\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}24xy\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}24xy\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}24xy\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[24xyz]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}24xy(4-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(96xy-24{x}^{2}y-24x{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(96xy-24{x}^{2}y-24x{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(96xy-24{x}^{2}y-24x{y}^{2})\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[48x{y}^{2}-12{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8x{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(128x-48{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(128x-48{x}^{2})\\,dx & =[64{x}^{2}-16{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(64-16)-0 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194315 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278071 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198619 1071 "![](images/01/0185149b5af7fc3c8d7b69be6bef4aa6364d3a8f27a3c9730dfdedeca72926f9.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)5x^{2}dV$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198619 1071 "![](images/01/0185149b5af7fc3c8d7b69be6bef4aa6364d3a8f27a3c9730dfdedeca72926f9.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}5{x}^{2}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198619 1074 "![](images/f8/f8704aa6c4823f6028caf14e5eef44a5a739d45476911930ae69daafc2ed05f7.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-\\frac{4x}{5}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31f2ff96afa1248427c1a6dd5074c47190c70983d7c0d5dfdd3def8bd883aef8.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-\\frac{4x}{5}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}5{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[5{x}^{2}z]_{z=0}^{z=3-4x/5}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}(3-\\frac{4x}{5})\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dx & =2[5{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =2(135-81)-0 \\\\  & =108.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194321 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278064 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198620 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}}3xyz\\,dz]dA\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,x-1\\le y\\le x+1}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198620 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$∬_(D)[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}}3xyzdz]dA$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x \\le 2, x - 1 \\le y \\le x + 1}$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198620 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}}3xyz\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}{[\\frac{3}{2}xy{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3}{2}xy({(\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}})}^{2}-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3}{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{2y}{x}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{x-1}^{x+1}3{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{x-1}^{x+1}3{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{y}^{3}]_{y=x-1}^{y=x+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+1)^{3}-(x-1)^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)^{4}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(81-16)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194327 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278147 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198621 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186198621 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$F(x, y, z) = ⟨e^{x}, e^{y}, z + 1⟩$,\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z =-x - y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le y \\le 1$." 13194139533404 17592186045585 false] [17592186198621 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩{|}_{z=-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨{{{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{{e}^{y}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1-x-y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({e}^{x})\\cdot (-1)-({e}^{y})\\cdot (-1)+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{x}y+{e}^{y}+y-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+e+1-x-\\frac{1}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}-x-\\frac{1}{2}+e)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+ex]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+e-1 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186233545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278033 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275484 1081 "8-3e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275484 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275484 1084 #uuid "0002a4f4-3c26-5341-91cd-bc4fba717d2e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275485 1081 "t\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup (2,\\infty )" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275485 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275485 1084 #uuid "002979da-748a-5d37-a335-76e888a20ce6" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275486 1081 "3u-3v" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275486 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275486 1084 #uuid "002ddc9f-2c71-5478-8c1d-77a6a8c334bf" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275487 1081 "2" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275487 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275487 1084 #uuid "0038ddda-e455-5e22-b133-45f5afd6bfa8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275488 1081 "images/cd/cdd115f6defaabc72aaaeb8a6a1642e088860f281a847acacdb635f71a26be08.png" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275488 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275488 1084 #uuid "005be913-c4c4-5c8a-94ae-a52d1df508fd" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186275489 1081 "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true