d "fe9e60e7-7f96-5949-baeb-94c3929775d1" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275662 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275849 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276246 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276308 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276831 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1077 "selection" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1079 #edb/ref 17592186276246 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278183 1080 #uuid "ffe754a0-0b7a-5223-83c1-87193e3a93a5" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278048 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278184 1069 "q-158424" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278184 1071 "![](images/81/819fa17f44f669746ba0c82d2d986f1cd5955a1d7d8144eda6922c0cb7813c9c.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3},\\, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278184 1072 #uuid "bac4e6a1-90fc-5ecd-80e4-898747c5b764" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278184 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{15}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{15}]-[\\frac{{1}^{4}}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277798 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278185 1069 "q-158425" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278185 1071 "![](images/87/87c5e1b3307b2a42fff6a97408c0475dbe242bef4a9dbfbabcec407b3fa23ce1.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278185 1072 #uuid "f670c211-dff8-5382-9b62-e560f1a2abfc" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278185 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(1)^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278103 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278186 1069 "q-158427" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278186 1071 "![](images/ae/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278186 1072 #uuid "8ba85730-5b8d-5d4c-8799-b386e641d64f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278186 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277959 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278187 1069 "q-158428" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278187 1071 "![](images/6e/6e540dc9309f2a5d1753760b4050c049e380705914d7289b47319de0b0696a9a.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{3}-1),\\, & \\frac{1}{2}<x\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278187 1072 #uuid "50d76297-0f50-5089-b83a-de45e6a36bc4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278187 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some value of $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}4({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x]}_{1/2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{81}{64}-\\frac{3}{2}]-[\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{80}{64}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278022 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278188 1069 "q-158429" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278188 1071 "![](images/0c/0c30c392223857c77cfc8619f1fa0d4b7fca20af618bdd424570cc3ed712f38b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1,\\, & 1<x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278188 1072 #uuid "0715ac14-20ca-5be9-8d09-d1a1ccc33941" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278188 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-x]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[9-3]-[1-1] \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277773 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278189 1069 "q-15843" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278189 1071 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278189 1072 #uuid "48febb37-71be-5629-b005-ff375ac1902f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278189 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277761 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278190 1069 "q-158431" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278190 1071 "Compute $P(2<X<4)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{20}\\sqrt{x},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{20}({x}^{2}-1) & 1<x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278190 1072 #uuid "efe7119c-4a0a-5b6a-a4ef-18306e6ddef8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278190 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<4) & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{20}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-x]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}([\\frac{1}{3}(4)^{3}-(4)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}-(2)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(\\frac{64}{3}-4-\\frac{8}{3}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(\\frac{50}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278019 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278191 1069 "q-158436" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278191 1071 "Compute $P(0\\le X\\le 2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}x(x+1),\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}x & 1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278191 1072 #uuid "8a416229-b58d-58b1-a133-9fc7c0c01068" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278191 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{5}x(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{5}({x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}+{[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}([\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}])+\\frac{1}{12}[{2}^{2}-{1}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{5}{6})+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278050 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278192 1069 "q-158442" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278192 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k(x+1),\\, & 2<x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278192 1072 #uuid "c628a4a3-1d03-5b7a-9d8c-d548c59d8196" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278192 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}k(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}k(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =k{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =k([\\frac{9}{2}+3]-[\\frac{4}{2}+2]) \\\\  & =k(\\frac{9}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278046 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278193 1069 "q-158443" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278193 1071 "Compute $P(0<X\\le 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}({x}^{3}+1),\\, & -1\\le x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{24}x & 1\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278193 1072 #uuid "f2c51ecb-6d65-53cb-ba0b-d70d6940d33b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278193 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}({x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{24}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x]}_{0}^{1}+{[\\frac{1}{48}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}+1]-[\\frac{1}{4}(0)^{4}+0])+\\frac{1}{48}[{5}^{2}-{1}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{5}{4})+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277936 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278194 1069 "q-158444" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278194 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{k},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278194 1072 #uuid "c59ec2e4-5b69-5af1-8174-b799a8f18aa3" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278194 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{x}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{x}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2k}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{25}{2k}-\\frac{9}{2k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278149 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278195 1069 "q-158445" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278195 1071 "Compute $P(-1\\le X\\le 2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{20} & 1<x\\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278195 1072 #uuid "a608a503-5ced-54e0-8320-a8bcb9a436bd" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278195 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le X\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1}+{[\\frac{1}{20}x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}([\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+1]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}+(-1)])+\\frac{1}{20}(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\frac{8}{3})+\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}+\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277760 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278196 1069 "q-158446" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278196 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k-x,\\, & -1<x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278196 1072 #uuid "656b41a9-8a99-538b-a47b-37d10e0765ca" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278196 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-[-k-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =k+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277970 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278197 1069 "q-158447" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278197 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k},\\, & 1<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278197 1072 #uuid "c1741b28-dd7a-521f-bff9-3881fb7d5bb4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278197 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}([\\frac{64}{3}+4]-[\\frac{1}{3}+1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}(21+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278175 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278198 1069 "q-15845" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278198 1071 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278198 1072 #uuid "aceee4b9-7657-5a9b-99d8-00f2a88fd80d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278198 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the Cartesian equation of the circle is $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277920 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278199 1069 "q-158452" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278199 1071 "Compute $P(1\\le X<2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{27}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278199 1072 #uuid "3f7ffcbf-5410-503a-9b98-ac31fb532ee3" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278199 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X<2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{27}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}{[{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}([(2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{4}]-[(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}(8-4-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{27}\\cdot \\frac{13}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277979 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278200 1069 "q-158454" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278200 1071 "Compute $P(X\\ge 2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{34}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278200 1072 #uuid "fbf411b4-73f3-51f5-8909-de69d45fc83c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278200 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is identical to zero for all $x>3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3}{34}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{34}{\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{34}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{34}([\\frac{1}{4}(3)^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{34}([\\frac{81}{4}-9]-[4-\\frac{8}{3}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{34}\\cdot \\frac{119}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278145 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278201 1069 "q-158458" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278201 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $17a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278201 1072 #uuid "36a6e17d-f83c-53ad-b495-79ac2ba0a0eb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278201 1074 "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 \\\\ -34 & 68\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ -34 & 68 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n17a+\\frac{b}{c}=17(\\frac{3}{17})+\\frac{2}{1}=5.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277903 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278202 1069 "q-158468" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278202 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278202 1072 #uuid "3ba0d5a0-909a-5afd-930c-63e0cd27ecd8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278202 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 8{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 8{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =8({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=8(1)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 8.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $8.$ But before we conclude that $8$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=8.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)^{2}+8(1)^{2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $8.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277851 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278203 1069 "q-158526" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278203 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278203 1072 #uuid "e74167c4-c7fe-5e65-910c-e5deae2cc1bc" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278203 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{2}^{2},\\,-5{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277974 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278204 1069 "q-158528" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278204 1071 "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278204 1072 #uuid "e7e7954d-9da2-5615-9bcc-af43d080c381" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278204 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-7{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-7(0)^{2}+4(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277753 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278205 1069 "q-158529" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278205 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278205 1072 #uuid "e1c124bc-a836-51e9-a514-6920747fc0ad" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278205 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-4(1)=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-4.$ But before we conclude that $-4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-4.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278051 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278206 1069 "q-158533" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278206 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278206 1072 #uuid "7a98e5a2-f222-532b-be08-4de02d1187ab" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278206 1074 "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n6{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{2}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=3.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}+6(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $3.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278068 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278207 1069 "q-158536" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278207 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278207 1072 #uuid "e7714139-ef1f-551b-b703-2555d562d417" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278207 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/de/de056be6665a342dc8569e0db330743a96e08fa770798c94ec7dd0cc4e987d92.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,2)$ and $Q(-2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/c6/c6c1237dfa5f8fed76bad01466c94442d27825c4edab7dbc92a99b177af4a0b6.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $Q$ is an interior point of $A$ since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained in $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277895 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278208 1069 "q-158538" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278208 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x\\le 1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $Q(2,-2)$ is an interior point of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278208 1072 #uuid "c0d5bc0a-69ec-5558-bf1a-6901b6354f4d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278208 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the left of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/11/119fbce04df572e5b1c083432d0183da1cc04add5e4ad56ac7704af3cf8ee024.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(2,-2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7d/7dc96640505a272ec9701a9d155f7ed2809e6f6df29ac5327af6b2b2324fa162.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the equation $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality $x\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277899 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278209 1069 "q-158541" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278209 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\ge 3x-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n-