(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277829 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278215 1069 "q-158564" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278215 1071 "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2y}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278215 1072 #uuid "cbb2ddad-ba55-5c7d-8280-316182c4f2ac" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278215 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2y\\ge 0 \\\\ 2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277776 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278216 1069 "q-158567" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278216 1071 "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}/y}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278216 1072 #uuid "d95d1a0b-f0b3-5203-b742-463bd2cca0ce" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278216 1074 "Notice tha