he entire $xy$-plane." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277814 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278219 1069 "q-158574" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278219 1071 "Evaluate $z=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-1$ at the point $(3,{e}^{2}).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278219 1072 #uuid "d225e24f-f8fc-5851-bdaa-9e7972c2ed2f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278219 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(3,{e}^{2}),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y={e}^{2}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(3,{e}^{2})} & =(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-1){|}_{(3,{e}^{2})} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-1 \\\\  & =3(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)-1 \\\\  & =3(2)-1 \\\\  & =6-1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277766 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278220 1069 "q-158577" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278220 1071 "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278220 1072 #uuid "806bcc75-b225-5d86-8c9e-f99567365362" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278220 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0,\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277848 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278221 1069 "q-158588" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278221 1071 "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{y}^{3}-x+2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(5,-1).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278221 1072 #uuid "6e391d1a-8298-5c2b-8fbb-e2c14854c2e0" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278221 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(5,-1),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=-1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5,-1) & =(3{y}^{3}-x+2){|}_{(5,-1)} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{3}-5+2 \\\\  & =-3-5+2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278112 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278222 1069 "q-158591" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278222 1071 "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy+7}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-4,-5).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278222 1072 #uuid "1a10c57f-681f-57e8-b21b-1312730cce62" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278222 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-4,-5),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-5$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4,-5) & =\\sqrt[3]{xy+7}\\,{|}_{(-4,-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(-4)(-5)+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{20+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278078 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278223 1069 "q-158592" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278223 1071 "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278223 1072 #uuid "64dcbd76-8d54-5023-881a-a57cc6e8bf6c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278223 1074 "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{|}_{(-6,-\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277980 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278224 1069 "q-158603" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278224 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x>1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,1)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278224 1072 #uuid "2253cfff-ee0a-5c15-93af-d26f561ec17f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278224 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the right of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7dc932c43ed153f4ac1832ca136f9035d879ea1f54e01c69e7a032b7a53d4940.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/06/06fdba4e26ebad28ef3f63106b7bdd8cb115b91a5f0076370a27b12f05added3.png)\n\n \n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ satisfy $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $x>1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278176 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278225 1069 "q-158684" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278225 1071 "![](images/5d/5dccdb1f8957bb15d7b2215320c6e632819a647bf4dc350ff850876bae6298ec.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-3,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278225 1072 #uuid "ed33ac2f-6299-5342-b520-1399b2a77786" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278225 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/d5/d595e29d31743694c404b99c79c60e40b152d30083e633d4269ad4444be3f826.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/23/2336ff8a65dd397e8324384b9f9536f3c7d4ad2d76a81caeba28d7fe3ec229ef.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/26/26903667d45cecf81539025c4cda333129cf89600821b383f20d574c75e537b0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277846 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278226 1069 "q-158685" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278226 1071 "![](images/73/73f61de1a7165454