{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3y),$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+h+3y).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+h+3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3y)}{h}).\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278072 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278237 1069 "q-159585" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278237 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-xy,$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(0,\\pi ).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278237 1072 #uuid "e406b09c-bfce-50a6-b8a5-dd556a32ecbf" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278237 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-y\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\pi  \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278016 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278238 1069 "q-159589" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278238 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+2)^{3}},$ then $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278238 1072 #uuid "31a26b77-cde2-5225-9c1c-15176a7705f8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278238 1074 "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+2)^{3}},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+h+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}[\\frac{1}{h}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+h+2)^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+2)^{3}})].\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278041 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278239 1069 "q-159593" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278239 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)={3}^{xy},$ then $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278239 1072 #uuid "96b9d7fd-e6bd-5861-b4a2-0c2b1d143000" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278239 1074 "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={3}^{xy},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)={3}^{x(y+h)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{{3}^{x(y+h)}-{3}^{xy}}{h}).\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277981 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278240 1069 "q-159594" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278240 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}-y},$ then $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278240 1072 #uuid "53daed36-1f31-5b2b-8966-96a4d35c598d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278240 1074 "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}-y},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)=\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}-(y+h)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}-(y+h)}-\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}-y}}{h}).\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278045 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278241 1069 "q-159597" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278241 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-2y\\sqrt{x},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,e).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278241 1072 #uuid "ff776960-64d0-5d6e-aeb2-a63fd457dce4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278241 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-2y\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})-2y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-2y\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,e)} & =3\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =3-e\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277911 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278242 1069 "q-159599" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278242 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{7}y,$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278242 1072 #uuid "4d7d1dc5-2124-525b-9cc9-fe9b2cf1012d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278242 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{7}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{7}y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+{x}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y})+{x}^{7}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{y}+{x}^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,1/2)} & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+{1}^{7} \\\\  & =2e+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277771 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278243 1069 "q-159602" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278243 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=4y{x}^{2}-x{y}^{4},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(2,1).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278243 1072 #uuid "099a0674-bec3-5a03-b0cd-02423c11a6a6" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278243 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y{x}^{2}-x{y}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)-x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}\\cdot (1)-x\\cdot (4{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-4x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,1)} & =4\\cdot {2}^{2}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =16-8 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277816 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278244 1069 "q-159607" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278244 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y+{x}^{4}y,$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278244 1072 #uuid "761131e3-fd5c-5281-9b00-7e9bad552026" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278244 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y+{x}^{4}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡y)+{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (\\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)+{x}^{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1+4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277996 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278245 1069 "q-159613" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278245 1071 "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ given that $f(x,y)=xy+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278245 1072 #uuid "7c020eea-053d-5f14-b5a2-c9f697a99828" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278245 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot 1+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278131 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278246 1069 "q-159616" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278246 1071 "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278246 1072 #uuid "198d06c9-ec80-5c14-815c-c9ec68f7a2d2" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278246 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot (1)-y\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278029 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278247 1069 "q-159619" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278247 1071 "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x-8{y}^{2}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278247 1072 #uuid "8fe04cdb-3f03-5a9d-a32d-3dd7d276f6a2" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278247 1074 "The notation ${f}_{x}$ denotes the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x.$\n\nWe differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x-8{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x)-8{y}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277835 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278248 1069 "q-159623" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278248 1071 "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={e}^{2x+3y}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278248 1072 #uuid "5ed8a7e6-be3b-5de6-bfb2-8c47db49086f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278248 1074 "The notation ${f}_{y}$ denotes the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y.$\n\nWe differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum rule for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x+3y}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{3y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{3y})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3y}+{x}^{2}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3{e}^{2x+3y}+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277975 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278249 1069 "q-159656" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278249 1071 "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}y}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278249 1072 #uuid "424001c9-342e-51ae-ba3f-40228db99e48" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278249 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}y})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot y\\cdot 2x-y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}y}-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277743 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278250 1069 "q-159658" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278250 1071 "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1}).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278250 1072 #uuid "0833bc78-75ed-516b-b7f3-7df11e162117" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278250 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2xy-1}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}(2x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot (2x\\cdot 1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278122 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278251 1069 "q-159660" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278251 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278251 1072 #uuid "2942efcf-e503-5d5c-b252-e682ffa52f18" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278251 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},2) & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi +2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278010 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278252 1069 "q-159700" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278252 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278252 1072 #uuid "95a36042-f58f-5fde-b6a7-7ca45f1d6809" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278252 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x+y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{{sin}^{2}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{sin}^{2}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,\\pi /2)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277978 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278253 1069 "q-159709" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278253 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(0,2).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278253 1072 #uuid "8c6d2d99-f435-50ff-85c3-a966d3aa1af6" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278253 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot (y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(0,2)} & =\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{(2+0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278035 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278254 1069 "q-159713" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278254 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{x+y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{2(x+y)\\sqrt{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278254 1072 #uuid "c75dffc7-c60f-5338-92f4-7c0c6ff2bb72" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278254 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{x+y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x+y})}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y))}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\sqrt{x+y}-(3x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot 1)}{{(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6(x+y)-(3x+y)}{2\\sqrt{x+y}})}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x+5y}{2(x+y)\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=3x+5y.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277940 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278255 1069 "q-159714" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278255 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x{y}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}y}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2h(x,y)}{{x}^{3}y}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278255 1072 #uuid "ad571fe8-149e-54b6-a89f-66ae79db7481" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278255 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{2x{y}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}y-(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)}{({x}^{2}y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}y-(2x{y}^{2}-1)\\cdot 2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2xy}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-2{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy(1-x{y}^{2})}{{x}^{4}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-x{y}^{2})}{{x}^{3}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=1-x{y}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278100 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278256 1069 "q-159716" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278256 1071 "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x{y}^{3}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278256 1072 #uuid "7c4ebeea-f58f-5eb8-93aa-37b6d0eeb259" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278256 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))-{y}^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =((4{x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277852 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278257 1069 "q-159723" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278257 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-{y}^{2}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278257 1072 #uuid "485c686b-8a53-5bf0-8bc4-72a915a1ba56" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278257 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (x-{y}^{2})-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{y}^{2})}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y\\cdot (x-{y}^{2})-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-2{y}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy+2{x}^{2}y}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy(1+x)}{{(x-{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=2xy(1+x).\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278136 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278258 1069 "q-159728" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278258 1071 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$11$** | **$15$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$3$** | **$5$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278258 1072 #uuid "27efdb52-f12a-5c0b-8849-a9786bd4270d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278258 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$11$** | **$15$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$3$** | **$5$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278177 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278259 1069 "q-159732" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278259 1071 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$8$** | **$13$** | **$22$** | **$27$** | **$35$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $9$ | $12$ | $21$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278259 1072 #uuid "4949bd7a-e727-5806-96c5-17f3b81427d9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278259 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$8$** | **$13$** | **$22$** | **$27$** | **$35$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $9$ | $12$ | $21$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 22 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 27 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 35\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278040 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278260 1069 "q-159752" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278260 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},4).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278260 1072 #uuid "fd55c472-88b5-5201-8165-4111e38766b9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278260 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product, the chain, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)))+\\sqrt{y}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy))+\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x))+\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1+\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1/4,4)} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4)+1+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277790 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278261 1069 "q-159757" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278261 1071 "If $z=-9-\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278261 1072 #uuid "2e339dfa-5444-5946-8701-7661eae41839" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278261 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-9-\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=-9,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(-9) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-9-\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-9+\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(-9-\\text{i})+(-9+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-9+(-9))+\\text{i}(-1+1) \\\\  & =-18+\\text{i}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277735 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278262 1069 "q-159758" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278262 1071 "Given that $z=9+3\\text{i},$ calculate $z\\overset{―}{z}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278262 1072 #uuid "ba737df7-5524-50ff-9af1-1507a3bfabf5" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278262 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=9+3\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=9,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={9}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\  & =81+9 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=9+3\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=9-3\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(9+3\\text{i})(9-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={9}^{2}+(9)(3)\\text{i}+(-3)(9)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(3)^{2} \\\\  & =81+27\\text{i}-27\\text{i}-(-1)(9) \\\\  & =81+9 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277919 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278263 1069 "q-159760" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278263 1071 "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278263 1072 #uuid "0a98d338-0c74-5c5f-9d1e-e0a133debca0" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278263 1074 "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{1}{4}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277987 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278264 1069 "q-159762" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278264 1071 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278264 1072 #uuid "b6efb939-92b8-5a77-8f54-81cda5e95098" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278264 1074 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we compute the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/db/dbe3773dea369f6b58fc2ad45a32f98c2c9ae356701baf3ab56af3daa6ca732d.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a reflection across the $x$-axis." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277888 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278265 1069 "q-159766" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278265 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(2,\\sqrt{5}).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278265 1072 #uuid "3f9cc492-b3ba-556f-a019-182cbc410bcf" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278265 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain, the sum, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & =1\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot ({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot ({x}^{2}\\cdot 0+2y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\sqrt{5}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\sqrt{5})} & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}+\\frac{{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5}+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{4+5}} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278156 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278266 1069 "q-159768" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278266 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(2,\\pi ).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278266 1072 #uuid "e7a86e6a-207f-552d-b9da-b1e5c673da4e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278266 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =x(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} & =2\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1-\\pi \\cdot 0)+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278020 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278267 1069 "q-159784" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278267 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278267 1072 #uuid "2ffee0ea-a323-5b51-850e-98d4c4b547d9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278267 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278011 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278268 1069 "q-159801" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278268 1071 "![](images/d9/d9ec98b03009d1023668aa12de88ed5515f0ea6df058d1b5d17804bf42082c5c.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown above. Which of the following is $△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′},$ the image of $△ABC$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278268 1072 #uuid "1333855f-29ce-5600-8e32-dc2d38172ec9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278268 1074 "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $△ABC:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}$ are $(6,4),(1,-6),(-3,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/4c/4c43c4c793924d4fde32b59c93b7a19be0109a389b126665868396b4c06d721e.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277744 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278269 1069 "q-159803" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278269 1071 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$0$** | **$14$** | **$-3$** | **$19$** | **$-1$** |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $5$ | $13$ | $21$ | $26$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278269 1072 #uuid "7c599aae-b76b-54d9-b3d6-320195ab6566" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278269 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$0$** | **$14$** | **$-3$** | **$19$** | **$-1$** |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $5$ | $13$ | $21$ | $26$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 19 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278021 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278270 1069 "q-159975" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278270 1071 "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278270 1072 #uuid "e3579af1-4491-5958-8694-2b4f12dcd55e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278270 1074 "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278106 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278271 1069 "q-159977" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278271 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,4),(0,2),$ and $(7,10).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(2,3)$\n- $(1,0)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278271 1072 #uuid "13406d62-3495-5617-8c59-07fc2d20bdc1" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278271 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,1),(-1,2),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277881 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278272 1069 "q-159978" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278272 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(7,5),$ and $(2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(0,0)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278272 1072 #uuid "b0b402cc-9d61-5ea9-b280-42fd73163096" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278272 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{9-8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(1,1),(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277995 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278273 1069 "q-160005" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278273 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278273 1072 #uuid "892c0064-22f4-5978-a464-d408a8aa051c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278273 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:  \n\n$$\n(x-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\n   \n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277797 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278274 1069 "q-160006" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278274 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278274 1072 #uuid "fd1c8424-6389-5af0-bbbd-63ebf8a65d67" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278274 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:  \n\n$$\n(x-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\n   \n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277868 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278275 1069 "q-160008" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278275 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278275 1072 #uuid "f58c3018-1078-5b3f-8bb8-2a1e7347605d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278275 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}-2x-\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+4-(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-4x+7) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x+14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278061 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278276 1069 "q-160016" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278276 1071 "Given a data set with $12$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=84,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278276 1072 #uuid "772997bf-eaba-51ee-be04-3ca467f838fb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278276 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=84,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 84=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278062 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278277 1069 "q-160026" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278277 1071 "Given that the mean of the following data set is $10,$ find the value of $b:$ \n\n$$\nb,\\,1,\\,b,\\,1,\\,b\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278277 1072 #uuid "88fbb37a-350b-51de-9b64-f5ff2acb9c93" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278277 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=5$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =b+1+b+1+b \\\\  & =2+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2+3b) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+3b}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{2+3b}{5} \\\\ 50 & =2+3b \\\\ 48 & =3b \\\\ b & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278181 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278278 1069 "q-160028" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278278 1071 "Given that the mean of the following data set is $11,$ find the value of $c:$ \n\n$$\n(c-3),\\,6,\\,3,\\,c,\\,10,\\,20\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278278 1072 #uuid "2ae95412-5543-5cd6-a29d-d4219b71ef9a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278278 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =(c-3)+6+3+c+10+20 \\\\  & =36+2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (36+2c) \\\\  & =\\frac{36+2c}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =\\frac{36+2c}{6} \\\\ 66 & =36+2c \\\\ 30 & =2c \\\\ c & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277741 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278279 1069 "q-160043" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278279 1071 "Given that $-4+\\text{i}=\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{2\\text{i}},$ calculate $p$ and $q.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278279 1072 #uuid "4c9db061-b78b-56b4-b368-a9d9c6ba6e09" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278279 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+\\text{i} & =\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{2\\text{i}} \\\\ (-4+\\text{i})(2\\text{i}) & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ -8\\text{i}+2{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ -2-8\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n-2=p.\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-2$ and $q=-8.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278099 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278280 1069 "q-160046" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278280 1071 "Given that $8\\text{i}=(p+\\text{i}q)(2+2\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278280 1072 #uuid "dac64397-57c8-51d9-9a92-cd614bf25b8f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278280 1074 "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $2+2\\text{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\text{i} & =(p+\\text{i}q)(2+2\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{8\\text{i}}{2+2\\text{i}} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\text{i}(2-2\\text{i})}{(2+2\\text{i})(2-2\\text{i})} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{16\\text{i}-16{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{2}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{16+16\\text{i}}{8} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 2+2\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $2=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $2=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=2$ and $q=2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278128 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278281 1069 "q-160050" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278281 1071 "Given that $1+7\\text{i}=(p+\\text{i}q)(3-4\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278281 1072 #uuid "349731a6-76ab-5448-ab2d-2501da4f5f67" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278281 1074 "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $3-4\\text{i}$ to isolate the\n  variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+7\\text{i} & =(p+\\text{i}q)(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{1+7\\text{i}}{3-4\\text{i}} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(1+7\\text{i})(3+4\\text{i})}{(3-4\\text{i})(3+4\\text{i})} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{3+21\\text{i}+4\\text{i}+28{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{3}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{-25+25\\text{i}}{9+16} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{-25+25\\text{i}}{25} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ -1+\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $-1=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $1=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278030 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278282 1069 "q-160052" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278282 1071 "A college basketball player scored the following number of points in the last five games he played:\n\n$$\n9,\\,11,\\,12,\\,8,\\,10\n$$\n\nWhat is the mean of these scores?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278282 1072 #uuid "b4975910-186b-51eb-8386-78fc12db0a69" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278282 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=5$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =9+11+12+8+10 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277791 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278283 1069 "q-160054" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278283 1071 "A car salesman has sold the following quantities of a particular model of car in the last few weeks:\n\n$$\n3,\\,5,\\,6,\\,8,\\,5,\\,3\n$$\n\nWhat is the mean of these figures?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278283 1072 #uuid "ac9d78d0-acf8-5125-b9f8-810932b0249e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278283 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =3+5+6+8+5+3 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277843 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278284 1069 "q-160055" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278284 1071 "Mark jogs every day in the neighborhood park. Last week he ran the following distances, in kilometers:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\text{km},\\,3\\,\\text{km},\\,2\\,\\text{km},\\,3\\,\\text{km},\\,1\\,\\text{km},\\,1\\,\\text{km},\\,3\\,\\text{km}\n$$\n\nWhat is the mean distance Mark jogged last week?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278284 1072 #uuid "403389ba-5e6f-51f5-9d50-8b073198a0a3" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278284 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=7$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} & =1+3+2+3+1+1+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278095 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278285 1069 "q-160056" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278285 1071 "What is the mean of the following data set?\n\n$$\n5,\\,3,\\,4,\\,6,\\,2,\\,4\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278285 1072 #uuid "06549be8-ec10-52cd-8838-9aee458ee847" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278285 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =5+3+4+6+2+4 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278183 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278286 1069 "q-160272" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278286 1071 "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=9.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278286 1072 #uuid "9395c592-0093-56e3-8f31-66b89d9022a7" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278286 1074 "When the circle intercepts the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$\n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (0-4)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ 16+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y+4)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y+4)^{2}=-7.$ Therefore, the circle does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point, as shown below.\n\n![](images/24/2442ca38770f2f82e2e9c536632e8474adb75e04504a4273987989bb3d81b282.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277874 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278287 1069 "q-160275" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278287 1071 "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278287 1072 #uuid "63117c35-29f3-5b55-92d7-7f60e9b36adc" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278287 1074 "When the circle intercepts the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$\n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (0+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y+2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y+2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the circle does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point, as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86f198b30096cf3106186ee2595b61c21be000e8e6223c5b42ee453ba1b9fe4a.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277818 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278288 1069 "q-160286" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278288 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+8x-6y+21=0$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278288 1072 #uuid "d6d8c38a-8409-54d7-9716-18a371d22f28" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278288 1074 "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+8x-6y+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}+8x-6\\cdot 0+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+21 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+16+21 & =0+16 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+21 & =16 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+4)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+4)^{2}=-5.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53b8e33665ee3ba0b36d04c9d82715b18505c501db535ecf4a40c48c81814584.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278084 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278289 1069 "q-160368" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278289 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,0),(-1,0),(2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(2,1)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278289 1072 #uuid "bf3cb2bb-bb5c-564c-af6e-ef65a9ae1e35" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278289 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(-1,0),(-2,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278105 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278290 1069 "q-160369" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278290 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,5),(4,-3),(3,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(2,5)$\n- $(-4,-3)$\n- $(-3,-2)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278290 1072 #uuid "4fb94a38-de9a-5a83-b5a3-84ca0f8fdab4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278290 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,-5),(-4,3),(-3,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277912 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278291 1069 "q-160371" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278291 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(6,-1),(-4,1),(3,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-6,-1)$\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(3,-5)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278291 1072 #uuid "213f7139-636c-56af-8c2a-7163cc744332" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278291 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,1),(4,-1),(-3,-5).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277817 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278292 1069 "q-160374" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278292 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,-3),(2,-2),(1,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278292 1072 #uuid "ff0f96a5-d30c-508e-bdde-b67be8bf18d4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278292 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,3),(-2,2),(-1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277957 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278293 1069 "q-160381" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278293 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278293 1072 #uuid "680f7a12-0c16-5883-aa06-5376f25863fd" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278293 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277827 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278294 1069 "q-160383" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278294 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278294 1072 #uuid "2b1f8f7b-fd82-5c84-a276-7fa2fd9b071a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278294 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot (-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277961 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278295 1069 "q-160396" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278295 1071 "Which of the following is an Argand diagram representing the complex number $z=-2?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278295 1072 #uuid "e6c4b38e-1b30-5e24-be7a-057bdc8f48f9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278295 1074 "We are given the complex number $z=-2.$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-2,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the point are $(-2,0)$, as shown in the Argand diagram below.\n\n![](images/55/556993791d4f77a087930583b910b06c92248b9a883d1c1358cfd6b081c572fb.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277885 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278296 1069 "q-160399" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278296 1071 "Which of the following numbers lie in the fourth quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $3+4\\text{i}$\n- $-2+3\\text{i}$\n- $1-2\\text{i}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278296 1072 #uuid "0ef2dcb7-ec09-542d-a947-352534dabf78" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278296 1074 "- The complex number $3+4\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=3$ and the imaginary part $y=4.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(3,4)$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2+3\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=3.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,3)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $1-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=1$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(1,-2)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n![](images/a4/a4f87e680d46b099b9543b552191c776ac7145fba4e1b51b10c44a3685907d46.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277944 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278297 1069 "q-160402" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278297 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278297 1072 #uuid "2443aa6d-e746-5170-93b3-e30684a9a33b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278297 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u-v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u-v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})-(-{e}^{u-v})\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{v}+\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}+\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}=2.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278069 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278298 1069 "q-160403" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278298 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278298 1072 #uuid "9c12a73a-3560-5ca0-8c93-5fcc98faad4d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278298 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{v}-\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277831 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278299 1069 "q-160405" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278299 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ 4u+6v\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278299 1072 #uuid "18d1f220-8bd2-586b-90ca-87751084600f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278299 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277871 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278300 1069 "q-160419" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278300 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\pi .$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $y=\\pi $" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278300 1072 #uuid "c7b0ec58-a026-543f-8622-101c6f9cdc96" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278300 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi ,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi .$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0+\\pi =\\pi \\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\pi $ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi +\\pi =0-\\pi +\\pi =0.$$\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277983 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278301 1069 "q-160422" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278301 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=1.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-1$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278301 1072 #uuid "09328241-3b39-5707-a62b-8ca38e3a3a9c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278301 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1)=1-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278031 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278302 1069 "q-160427" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278302 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278302 1072 #uuid "861c1236-2662-5b7d-b150-03b6774d0f17" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278302 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278090 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278303 1069 "q-160428" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278303 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278303 1072 #uuid "19415143-780a-5182-8164-c1af6838225c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278303 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277994 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278304 1069 "q-160431" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278304 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278304 1072 #uuid "876a5d15-9cbd-5150-8246-c71dbabaa45c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278304 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277822 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278305 1069 "q-160432" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278305 1071 "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+2}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278305 1072 #uuid "178456d5-a9a8-5682-8678-ab188a60d356" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278305 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+2},$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{c}^{4}+2} & =0 \\\\ {c}^{4}+2 & =0 \\\\ {c}^{4} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that ${c}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the above equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the differential equation has no equilibrium points." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278146 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278306 1069 "q-160440" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278306 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278306 1072 #uuid "4f68c62f-9f11-5647-83b4-1238f13388b9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278306 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277887 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278307 1069 "q-160444" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278307 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y(t)=-1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y(t)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278307 1072 #uuid "9bb7ec9e-9e63-5c70-a122-192889149887" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278307 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+1=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}+3(1)^{2}+3(1)+1=8\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+1)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+1)(y+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\ny(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277931 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278308 1069 "q-160447" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278308 1071 "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x/2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278308 1072 #uuid "b24e08c4-4299-5d2f-a3af-dcce0689b7c1" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278308 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x/2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x/2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & =2({e}^{0}-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277774 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278309 1069 "q-160450" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278309 1071 "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278309 1072 #uuid "f0b61c31-2e3e-5b9a-9684-a3b763cdba2b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278309 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278126 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278310 1069 "q-160455" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278310 1071 "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{1-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278310 1072 #uuid "e9f1f757-0f29-5257-92da-0c605e690da7" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278310 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=2{e}^{1-2x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }2{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2x}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277842 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278311 1069 "q-160459" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278311 1071 "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278311 1072 #uuid "3e574537-84e0-52ce-b8da-3afe1320a385" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278311 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 0,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278065 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278312 1069 "q-160460" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278312 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278312 1072 #uuid "cb96d7dc-7f59-56fa-90d1-c2a71b2fab70" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278312 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-(-2v)\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=0$ defines the point $(0,0)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278003 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278313 1069 "q-160463" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278313 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}},\\, & x\\ge -2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278313 1072 #uuid "e4ee7fd8-9373-59cf-bc78-d62531a54190" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278313 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}$ on the interval $x\\ge -2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{k}{6(x+3)^{6}}\\,{|}_{-2}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277921 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278314 1069 "q-160464" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278314 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{(2x-1)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278314 1072 #uuid "03556609-63a0-5bb8-aeec-cc678726996a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278314 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(2x-1)^{2}}$ on the interval $x\\ge 2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(2x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(2x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{k}{2(2x-1)}\\,{|}_{2}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277763 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278315 1069 "q-160466" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278315 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{1-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278315 1072