592186278320 1069 "q-160581" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278320 1071 "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-x+7y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278320 1072 #uuid "ed8207bc-4d78-5a97-b503-5177675ffb34" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278320 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=-x+7y$ and $v(x,y)=3x-y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3-7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277993 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278321 1069 "q-160582" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278321 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278321 1072 #uuid "1077730f-94dd-5875-b9c2-642bfe60bb08" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278321 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y{e}^{xy} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{e}^{xy}}{x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278139 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278322 1069 "q-160599" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278322 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278322 1072 #uuid "bcef19dc-e0d9-5a18-86b4-519865d6c64e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278322 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \nHere, we have $u(x,y)=xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{y} \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278027 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278323 1069 "q-160602" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278323 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278323 1072 #uuid "ad2c8de4-8a2b-5de9-ac83-ed83d956c1b8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278323 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,u) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{0}^{2},\\,u) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277762 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278324 1069 "q-160604" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278324 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}-u,\\,y=uv.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278324 1072 #uuid "fbc1cc5f-b781-5ba3-8388-72105273d004" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278324 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}-u,\\,uv) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-u,\\,u(1)) \\\\  & =(1-u,\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278153 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278325 1069 "q-160607" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278325 1071 "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(u+v),\\,y=\\frac{2u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278325 1072 #uuid "235488a1-420f-5270-8713-f985503b0b2d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278325 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u(u+v),\\,\\frac{2u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =(u(u+0),\\,\\frac{2u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx={(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=4x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0(0+0),\\,\\frac{2(0)}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1(1+0),\\,\\frac{2(1)}{0+1})=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$\n\n![](images/4f/4fc3c209e83fc1d6b4c9258cdb739fb0a73db797791cf8fcca2e51a7592d54f8.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277841 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278326 1069 "q-160617" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278326 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,2),(1,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(-1,3)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278326 1072 #uuid "61d3fab2-038b-5d24-9a0c-708feb5787d8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278326 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(0,-1),(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277927 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278327 1069 "q-160618" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278327 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(2,-3),(1,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(2,1)$\n- $(-2,3)$\n- $(-1,5)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278327 1072 #uuid "db002c05-0ad9-5e9a-a3e6-2b51920ba6fb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278327 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,1),(-2,3),(-1,-5).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277937 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278328 1069 "q-160619" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278328 1071 "![](images/f3/f3e31d1c15a97adea32790eafe75ad1bc9f70c854b516b98de6648ec8d7bcb87.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278328 1072 #uuid "ecda41b1-c814-51d0-b755-d1273a4b443b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278328 1074 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/9f/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae800b271848948b5f60f5cd7c1cecd163c2aa7399e9a87e2a52d158857c6a42.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278107 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278329 1069 "q-160620" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278329 1071 "![](images/b7/b71b6c7e76bfecb29d77c5bf38fba4db3fdafb5b2f5e272ffd123b95b10b162e.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278329 1072 #uuid "418553f0-b8c7-5549-a68a-8a67cc2b4132" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278329 1074 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned