4d8ba90c02463fbf96525b1cd851bec685a005a34834f81.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277738 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278332 1069 "q-160629" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278332 1071 "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278332 1072 #uuid "abca845b-bdf8-5f44-9d9f-d46002292324" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278332 1074 "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+0,\\,{0}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v+1,\\,{v}^{2}-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,{1}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,1-{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/61/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278089 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278333 1069 "q-160635" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278333 1071 "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=uv,\\,y=-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278333 1072 #uuid "b37ce52d-e566-5093-8b6a-19371d4f2b74" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278333 1074 "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/5c/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [175921862