, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(1-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(0(v-0),\\,-0) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0).$\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/c9/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277788 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278335 1069 "q-160643" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278335 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,0),(-1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(-2,1)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278335 1072 #uuid "11e8f9e6-6315-5288-8908-c9cd51236438" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278335 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(0,1),(-2,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277891 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278336 1069 "q-160644" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278336 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-3),(3,1),(-4,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(1,-3)$\n- $(-1,4)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278336 1072 #uuid "932ec8a6-f54a-5a6e-82db-611776aadb8f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278336 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,-2),(1,-3),(-1,4).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278074 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278337 1069 "q-160645" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278337 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,1),(1,-4),(1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,3)$\n- $(4,1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278337 1072 #uuid "5b1a15ea-ca58-59ce-bcaf-a775bc97fe15" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278337 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-3),(4,1),(0,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278119 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278338 1069 "q-160647" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278338 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(5,-1),(2,0),(-2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,-5)$\n- $(0,2)$\n- $(-1,-2)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278338 1072 #uuid "d53ec269-7021-530a-b0ec-0053da9223ca" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278338 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-5),(0,-2),(-1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277985 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278339 1069 "q-160648" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278339 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(1,-1),(-2,3),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-3,-2)$\n- $(0,2)$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278339 1072 #uuid "97308e70-f67e-5dc9-9884-0142720ba3a3" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278339 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,1),(-3,-2),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277839 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278340 1069 "q-160769" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278340 1071 "What is the value of \n $f(s)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡s\\,i+\\operatorname{cot}⁡s\\,j-\\,k$ at $s=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278340 1072 #uuid "7b01d56e-0cd6-5466-b9de-28d868176a07" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278340 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute at $s=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-k \\\\  & =1\\,i+1\\,j-k \\\\  & =i+j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of  $f(s)$ at $s=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  is $i+j-k.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277804 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278341 1069 "q-160776" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278341 1071 "What is the value of $f(t)=2{e}^{t}\\,i+\\sqrt{t+4}\\,j+(t-2)^{4}\\,k$ at $t=0?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278341 1072 #uuid "9415c537-2e54-5d27-abb1-94edbbabb029" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278341 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $t=0$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2{e}^{0}\\,i+\\sqrt{0+4}\\,j+(0-2)^{4}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+16\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $f(t)$ at $t=0$ is the vector $2\\,i+2\\,j+16\\,k.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277809 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278342 1069 "q-160780" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278342 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2)\\,i+\\sqrt{t}\\,j+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278342 1072 #uuid "d55085b0-923c-5594-8b36-76442175a849" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278342 1074 "To find the domain of $f,$ we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2)$ is $t\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{t}$ is $t\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/33/33e19c4978f1a0975b6d583f127ff593500d2563acbef5a7cf76b2f6bfaef070.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278171 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278343 1069 "q-160788" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278343 1071 "What is the value of $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t+1} \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$ at $t=0?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278343 1072 #uuid "b8df2535-af98-5f9d-b645-9edd76ae78d4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278343 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $t=0$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(0)+1} \\\\ 1-(0)^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the value of $f(t)$ at $t=0$ is $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277967 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278344 1069 "q-160794" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278344 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{3-t},\\,\\sqrt{t+2},\\,{e}^{2t}⟩.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278344 1072 #uuid "7fe65d76-24c5-5e27-9204-6acc9e8e6e0f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278344 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\frac{1}{3-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\sqrt{t+2}$ is $t\\in [-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${e}^{2t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/39/39a4299fea0f2137e79490cce9e4660f0c1b021f0de9977090cc3a572df41b1f.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in [-2,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277750 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278345 1069 "q-160861" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278345 1071 "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n- The set is closed under addition." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278345 1072 #uuid "d50910b7-4560-52e1-8087-133e3875c50e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278345 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278118 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278346 1069 "q-160866" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278346 1071 "Let $W$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in W.$\n- The set $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278346 1072 #uuid "c5fa4f79-ef60-5ce2-8e28-cc585f25eefe" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278346 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)=(0)^{2}+1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple not in $W.$ For example, let $p(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $p(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $p(0)=1.$ However, \n\n$$\n2\\cdot p(0)=2\\cdot ({0}^{2}+1)=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n Hence, $k\\cdot p(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278001 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278347 1069 "q-160872" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278347 1071 "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- ${I}_{2}\\in R$\n- For every $A,B\\in R,$ we have $A+B\\in R.$\n- The set is closed under addition." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278347 1072 #uuid "94165333-3657-56e4-b088-f39008c588be" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278347 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in R$ since \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwith $a=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There are matrices in $R$ whose sum is *not* in $R.$ For example, let $A=B={I}_{2}.$ Then $A,B\\in R,$  however,\n\n$$\nA+B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\notin R,\n$$\n\nsince the $11$-entry of this resulting matrix is equal to  $2\\ne 1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278104 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278348 1069 "q-160874" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278348 1071 "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $g(x)\\in C,$ where $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- For every $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n- The set is closed under addition." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278348 1072 #uuid "e68dc301-469b-5da0-9b57-a516f231e45b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278348 1074 "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials whose degree is less than or equal to $2,$ however\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡({x}^{3})=3>2.\n$$\n\nSo, $g(x)={x}^{3}\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ then $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 2$ and $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))\\le 2.$ Since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)$ is less than or equal to the maximum of the degrees of $p$ and $q$, we deduce that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277747 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278349 1069 "q-160910" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278349 1071 "![](images/ad/ad0e3ddbeb228e3fdeeb09823493fb54a4a7f930c25ecd3b69b71dcf77e1d923.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278349 1072 #uuid "d06b5b15-7a3e-5904-b5c5-3cae4271a2e8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278349 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar 