tered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.