ven by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278353 1072 #uuid "f157d340-55fe-5182-98c8-272f8763a3d4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278353 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b829ff7e7a51ed9f8089b0b8fc22f727ccf5dc7b2e937ec90f6a1affb8d29064.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277836 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278354 1069 "q-160951" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278354 1071 "![](images/81/81ccd8830bcafa56065d5f7481e0d0b1dc114d0cd67a9d04220c8bb4459968d3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278354 1072 #uuid "9ddab065-40f7-50dd-8567-62810094f305" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278354 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=0$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/96/968e682dbaa9d8bdb95808e609a1dba6c68e8e12d9cae1abb59755b5ec640286.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277786 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278355 1069 "q-160962" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278355 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x$\n- ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$\n- $a=0$ and $b=2$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278355 1072 #uuid "a2294020-eb10-5fa0-b942-17d14bfa1ac7" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278355 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{2x}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/0b/0bcadeca7ede6f709bf06391e10da66afd1b514fd0e041bbf108e6e4229b0da6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277784 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278356 1069 "q-160967" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278356 1071 "![](images/21/21952fe273fd1a1ecb03727f4aa254d4e0ecdab2feaeb1d8c652bc9923404846.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278356 1072 #uuid "019ee70c-3708-5fe3-b8b1-31b6dfb8e888" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278356 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the third quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{9},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277884 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278357 1069 "q-161026" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278357 1071 "![](images/70/708bf4df2d45383b0dd367f5bfe3dc9e260a17cfefe0566e2b6d37dec98af2ef.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278357 1072 #uuid "a3b231b2-8f67-5351-80e4-0c74d7566eae" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278357 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $0\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})),\\,0\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d3/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278059 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278358 1069 "q-161028" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278358 1071 "![](images/aa/aa62dda1abb7bc6bca1b2e6cdf356b52946820912ec6318e5901273a546ee96a.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278358 1072 #uuid "e82f4301-a070-5bc8-9a49-2d57b54ecb56" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278358 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277877 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278359 1069 "q-161030" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278359 1071 "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{__________},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1].$ What is the second component of $r?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278359 1072 #uuid "fb5298c5-b27e-57d5-b45f-5e57c46f1353" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278359 1074 "![](images/04/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$  \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is ${e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277849 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278360 1069 "q-161031" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278360 1071 "![](images/09/0963fc3c56b5baac68e2d587896bf4655950640ecce5b924edc2d8e26a3e64e2.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278360 1072 #uuid "507c7b5b-1f55-5451-94d5-1770ddfeea4a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278360 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(u,\\,0),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/92/92bd315a07a5d76e77c249c12ea4544ee1555b0c214c68cf1f92fc3f07a71ddf.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $0$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point \n$(0,\\,0),$\nwhich is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{3},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies on the circle with radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278012 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278361 1069 "q-161032" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278361 1071 "![](images/16/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278361 1072 #uuid "4b7f9af3-cd62-5b44-9828-72b23454ff87" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278361 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278002 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278362 1069 "q-161035" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278362 1071 "![](images/8a/8afe0884ccf1e62ffdfef6cab4626bc59b3138aa5cd26f855980c88971753447.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278362 1072 #uuid "35901d5d-b737-59bd-bd22-20c686ac3fe1" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278362 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b836799bc512db70d2ce5fcfe70db2af3b2572d584d317fa5ed515b363a11ddc.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point $(0,\\,0),$ which is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{5},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies in the circle with radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277812 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278363 1069 "q-161036" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278363 1071 "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\text{__________},\\,({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={2}^{z}-1$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,2].$ What is the first component of $r?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278363 1072 #uuid "6a403ba5-6191-5834-b839-0ec325e58908" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278363 1074 "![](images/04/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)={2}^{z}-1\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,2].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [0,2],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $({2}^{z}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277863 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278364 1069 "q-161038" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278364 1071 "![](images/aa/aa5b04b98d3796656fca912d8dc1732ea7e12bab9083f8ca543ad5fd4fdb10ff.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278364 1072 #uuid "ebe3923c-2578-5c4c-8b9f-d97199b48c08" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278364 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277901 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278365 1069 "q-161039" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278365 1071 "![](images/cd/cdd5f0c58228b47a0bbba77daf2b426ce0247e71259e2b5af1a4a80fa833a036.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278365 1072 #uuid "e368e6ad-9cc6-5a3e-aa55-7ffed535a21a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278365 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277799 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278366 1069 "q-161049" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278366 1071 "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278366 1072 #uuid "aff73867-9b1f-5562-9960-420de4061c93" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278366 1074 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{sin}^{3}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\frac{1}{3}{sin}^{3}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(1-\\frac{1}{3})-0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277755 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278367 1069 "q-161050" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278367 1071 "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=15{x}^{2}y$ over the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278367 1072 #uuid "9fa6b6f5-4249-530e-aeb1-5511d96e1746" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278367 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/9b/9b54b7a488a1983ba1816fdf5c55e039951eb417102c61963977b2f48b9c8d9e.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-({cos}^{3}⁡(2\\pi )-{cos}^{3}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277892 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278368 1069 "q-161051" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278368 1071 "$$\n⟨\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278368 1072 #uuid "4bd35fbf-98e6-549b-868f-29cc78eccc1f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278368 1074 "Notice that $x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y),\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278168 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278369 1069 "q-161052" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278369 1071 "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=4{y}^{2}$ over the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278369 1072 #uuid "ad9dc383-588b-5128-942d-8b56b5fb2221" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278369 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (81-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278120 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278370 1069 "q-161053" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278370 1071 "Integrate the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ over the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278370 1072 #uuid "bba8bb12-bb44-5085-86bc-e29c01145558" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278370 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/81/811cfa127ad232e8d5a990998604718a57344d1bd7f43173dc0987631c13db33.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278075 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278371 1069 "q-161054" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278371 1071 "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ over the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278371 1072 #uuid "f641b0ca-15bb-53b1-96d0-217b8812f929" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278371 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278044 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278372 1069 "q-161055" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278372 1071 "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278372 1072 #uuid "c4a72efd-94bd-57c9-9125-f124ca8a7f81" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278372 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277923 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278373 1069 "q-161061" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278373 1071 "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278373 1072 #uuid "c1ab1d22-20a5-53e7-b1d4-b12bfa53d601" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278373 1074 "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi /4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277947 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278374 1069 "q-161063" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278374 1071 "$$\n⟨\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $3$rd component of the product?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278374 1072 #uuid "121067f9-6a02-54b7-8d8d-5663e9e8648d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278374 1074 "Notice that $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ and    \n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}