\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=(x-2)^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2(x-2).$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2(x-2)^{3},\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278173 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278380 1069 "q-161079" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278380 1071 "![](images/4d/4da25a7fcce468774aafcf2c1dbf7b4ef65089194d0330b1159d18a9a8f4bfe9.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278380 1072 #uuid "d548092f-8e97-501c-97b5-1d5ab43c5b4d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278380 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\op