ame{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277765 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278384 1069 "q-161099" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278384 1071 "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278384 1072 #uuid "8a98dcae-fa38-562f-8676-270d1756205a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278384 1074 "Notice that $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277746 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278385 1069 "q-161101" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278385 1071 "![](images/ae/aed70cc2e5495fcfd9f42ff5d79c3371fb17b62e12ab1909c7738071e59f1a23.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=x+2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278385 1072 #uuid "3f11e52b-1477-5101-9ca1-e393f690fd0d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278385 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=x+2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/b9/b9c2cd48302ef8d4e2d5e65a56d19506600c5e6552a4d9a1ef7d578a09a21557.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278166 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278386 1069 "q-161104" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278386 1071 "![](images/05/059cec995f7ef75b56b8c512af09051fe4631733e87d98d25065144500613f73.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $17{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278386 1072 #uuid "56305129-86bb-5e37-8747-ef7193f88a5c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278386 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{46}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 63\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{46}^{∘} \\\\  & =22.659… \\\\  & ≈23{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =23-17 \\\\  & =6{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277876 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278387 1069 "q-161107" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278387 1071 "![](images/ef/ef8379b21526455a723369fda4360e2d7d3ef3cf54ce7d7767df7bd160a169f4.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $5\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278387 1072 #uuid "1a46e83d-b704-567c-8d00-1886b9f6bc9b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278387 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 5\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}\\,b}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{5\\sqrt{3}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{5\\sqrt{3}}\\,b}{4} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{b}{4} \\\\ b & =4\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=4\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277948 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278388 1069 "q-161108" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278388 1071 "![](images/aa/aa8020019d441b741a9b621a0015fda31b3aa199afd8fc145aa840b1749e7a8b.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278388 1072 #uuid "ea13205f-869d-5677-b09a-f9a147ae7004" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278388 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab94778cea0a627b227e39745bc9efe3f516b5bcfb5560ee70d6fd30fa58289a.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278138 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278389 1069 "q-161109" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278389 1071 "![](images/b9/b95571772af958b4f7b7dcdbba630b78e029c838340dc272809a6c7915dc9c90.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278389 1072 #uuid "43fd23a0-08b8-58ee-b7e6-43240cfa05d7" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278389 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=6\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\,b}{2} \\\\ \\frac{21\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}}{\\cancel{2}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\,b}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ 3b & =21 \\\\ b & =7\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=7\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278170 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278390 1069 "q-161114" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278390 1071 "![](images/5b/5b5b5e82eb8dca9ae71c08b306ac4618500c7c3af4aa0858297d5585e055007f.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $4$ and $6,$ as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278390 1072 #uuid "4e1a8f74-9fd7-53da-9313-229356fec0da" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278390 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $4$ and $6$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ we obtain $r=6.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{6}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{6}\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[r]}_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (6-4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278108 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278391 1069 "q-161115" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278391 1071 "![](images/63/636c9e587d6cbb9fcf9b24d068520330d5365cf80a2800bdd8244698512be3c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $5,$ as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278391 1072 #uuid "06fc34bf-ed22-543d-8e30-2936b87d0b0d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278391 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{3/2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{r}]}_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278014 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278392 1069 "q-161117" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278392 1071 "![](images/7b/7bb6d2b9f9e09de44e078657ce3517bfd78261d3206743b7882abf0112f38a93.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, $z=0,$ and $z=3-x+y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278392 1072 #uuid "0b05250a-2126-54a5-8110-d92f7f2aa122" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278392 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y.$\n\n![](images/65/65e179e16b66009b9168c4380032b01c3a79b3c4a2afc3b8e5084defb77b85fc.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277861 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278393 1069 "q-161118" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278393 1071 "![](images/83/8360f06b0d9e9633aa00b779702d386cd403d7fe8ac8a28b51922dca92ddafed.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278393 1072 #uuid "dc9dfc88-f183-5e04-83ce-439434807dc2" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278393 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=0$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/83/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png)" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277964 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278394 1069 "q-161121" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278394 1071 "![](images/9c/9cd21fbe460f530f08bc8e85f407f9d6ac49b263d3a5361af093a21cd1ca8d11.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is a half annulus enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278394 1072 #uuid "60159fed-8119-56a1-9a58-db906033ca8e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278394 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the half-annulus between the disks of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/da/da18aafec278b5d44b0530097729e4cb4b319e219c005fc155d78fab8aa90937.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta <\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{625}{4}-\\frac{81}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{[-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =136\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{272}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277821 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278395 1069 "q-161125" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278395 1071 "![](images/e5/e5369264a9ab9328449bafd6fd3dcda1a70b7d90cbaf4f9815211e0cc3873fc0.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $4x+5z=15.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278395 1072 #uuid "99aeb9ca-3424-5f18-af86-89763375eaeb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278395 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}.$\n\n![](images/ed/edea8af334c3362f583fcb86709150690eeec79a54185b67d242bfa14a8e4c75.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277916 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278396 1069 "q-161126" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278396 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278396 1072 #uuid "128c01a3-ffea-59eb-9892-8068500c0400" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278396 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(4y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278005 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278397 1069 "q-161127" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278397 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u,\\,z=v\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278397 1072 #uuid "fb6920aa-28a6-5cb5-a583-f417d82bbe6a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278397 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u,\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-2yz\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}-2uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277976 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278398 1069 "q-161131" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278398 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278398 1072 #uuid "b62215b7-0e69-5d45-8968-8bb0a1bc12ef" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278398 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{3},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2})}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277926 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278399 1069 "q-161132" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278399 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{u}^{2},\\,2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278399 1072 #uuid "0739fb8b-67dd-5c96-8b51-b9fe22150a63" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278399 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xy+z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =u.{u}^{2}+2v \\\\  & ={u}^{3}+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278141 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278400 1069 "q-161134" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278400 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278400 1072 #uuid "3771f844-0efb-5152-bb73-0315652e05d3" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278400 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {u}^{2})\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\, \\\\  & ={u}^{4}{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{5}{v}^{4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278152 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278401 1069 "q-161136" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278401 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}+3yz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278401 1072 #uuid "af99ff54-0eba-5a3e-990a-a1878cebaaed" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278401 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-{x}^{2}+3yz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-(u\\cdot u)+3\\cdot (u+v)\\cdot ({v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278026 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278402 1069 "q-161137" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278402 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between  the plane $z=2y+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$  Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1$  and  ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278402 1072 #uuid "d909db1e-2d8a-5169-914f-b321d4c2af66" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278402 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto $xy$-plane.  \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278134 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278403 1069 "q-161139" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278403 1071 "Consider the portion of the surface $z=x+{y}^{3}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=1.$ Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278403 1072 #uuid "c11e996e-10b6-5010-bc4a-0d91257a3dfe" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278403 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x+{y}^{3},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/87/87845c65dba220978ca81bd5a2b27816ca15f951cd14d8fe499ab43d3c64ef77.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3})=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3})=3{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(1)^{2}+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277815 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278404 1069 "q-161140" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278404 1071 "Consider the portion of the surface $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278404 1072 #uuid "134aff90-1d55-5793-9da0-c59e5f46f5e8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278404 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf7a5921936b44a2e672fcee524c5a230800e6130f6c0a8683e6ce46829df58.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278162 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278405 1069 "q-161143" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278405 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between  the plane $z=2y+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le x\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2y+1$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$\n- $a=1$ and $b=2$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278405 1072 #uuid "91da0da4-8cfa-5b1f-811f-ea50a1876fb0" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278405 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,y)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=2y+1$ and *above* by $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{{2y+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,y)}\\le z\\le {{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto the $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.    \n\n![](images/39/39d2daceb171d26d3396e5aaeab4d8b8da2e9b8324e4042f2b7ed048f4a5fdeb.png)\n\nThe region is bounded on the *left* by $x=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and bounded on the *right* by $x=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,      \n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277969 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278406 1069 "q-161144" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278406 1071 "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278406 1072 #uuid "433fa395-a287-58c1-aa07-6bcf4cb6b740" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278406 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6200343142274ee2f5b3934cbe014ee24edf8177f3a544c38dc070bb7df83ef.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{cos}^{2}⁡x+8}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277867 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278407 1069 "q-161155" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278407 1071 "Find the surface area of the portion of the cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278407 1072 #uuid "2a3ad5a7-79b4-5ae2-b579-811466f8e968" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278407 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})^{2}+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277950 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278408 1069 "q-161156" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278408 1071 "Find the surface area of the portion of the cylinder $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278408 1072 #uuid "bbda2ff7-05a0-5600-96fa-c9dfe7d1924f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278408 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277918 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278409 1069 "q-161160" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278409 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}$\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278409 1072 #uuid "e71e8fdb-04f6-527e-89b1-2171dc56e3a9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278409 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6$ onto $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2y+6 & =8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=4$ centered at $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277894 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278410 1069 "q-161164" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278410 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2y-x$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9$ and $x\\le 0.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278410 1072 #uuid "bd23390c-f0cc-59ec-891d-3ea7fdf2c7b8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278410 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2y-x,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,x\\le 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c6c556ec90138fa1f0b599c6afed9d093e5c1b10f7751a5fbe55f0add617e1d.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277905 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278411 1069 "q-161165" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278411 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278411 1072 #uuid "95ffc39b-753c-5fd6-923e-0b13c7adc887" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278411 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d2774aa4a85d25c4e35f3eb327adc82f9ef02eceb2be31ddf730493797e4cac2.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2x)^{2}+(-2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 3 \\\\ u & 1 & 37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{37}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(37)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277952 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278412 1069 "q-161167" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278412 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278412 1072 #uuid "66ec1dd0-f28a-513c-a9f4-29ac282d0de0" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278412 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7cb4d16f39d1c1e38d65000471cbc5ca1831a0bbc2cfc61c4f03d087d6a5125.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=4-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r & =-\\pi {\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 2{({u}^{1/2})}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({4}^{1/2}-{3}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277992 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278413 1069 "q-161173" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278413 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the hyperbolic paraboloid $x={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraints ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9$ and $z\\le 0.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278413 1072 #uuid "b5e36073-07ed-5be9-b9f9-14a5dbeadf43" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278413 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9,\\,z\\le 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd5bcd7857769afb5f3a4f6197757c08eb295c7075a271c302c4b6087d661d14.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 3 \\\\ u & 1 & 37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}[(37)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277781 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278414 1069 "q-161174" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278414 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x=2-y-z$ that is subject to the constraints ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0,\\,z\\ge 0.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278414 1072 #uuid "7d00a6aa-63ec-5be5-aa3c-4e85a0b4a1af" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278414 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=2-y-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,z\\ge 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/37/3741c5db05232f04f60f747dc04176bfc77da30bbc39c79888cf4c18939369f7.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-y-z)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2-y-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTher