86278417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277850 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278417 1069 "q-161180" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278417 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+2y-z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,2u+2v,\\,2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278417 1072 #uuid "f3da3d1c-88b2-5b2c-b1c5-adc3acc48bde" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278417 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+2y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2u+2v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =(u-v)+2(2u+2v)-(2v) \\\\  & =5u+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(5u+v)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,(5u+v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+v)\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{5{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{[\\frac{5v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6[5+2] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277990 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278418 1069 "q-161182" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278418 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6xyz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278418 1072 #uuid "c6424aa2-1e21-5507-975b-b73e043b3939" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278418 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =6xyz\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =6(u)(u+v)(v) \\\\  & =6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}v}{3}+\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{v}{3}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277930 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278419 1069 "q-161184" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278419 1071 "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278419 1072 #uuid "a172a4da-f55b-58e5-9a2f-32af9ff9ae04" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278419 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b9/b9504140a4827cd74b74e8c23f76e1305389f1bc93a87834a37d4b20222e56f0.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(x)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277966 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278420 1069 "q-161187" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278420 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278420 1072 #uuid "79482994-a679-564f-a071-89c4e5f25898" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278420 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=3$ for  $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278096 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278421 1069 "q-161190" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278421 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,(2x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278421 1072 #uuid "ae22adfc-61f1-5211-b117-6cf267aa0468" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278421 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+1 & =2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2z-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the following ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277737 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278422 1069 "q-161192" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278422 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278422 1072 #uuid "d5ad771c-ffec-54eb-9e31-b5f8d4db3b56" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278422 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for  $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,4x,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277869 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278423 1069 "q-161194" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278423 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2y-x-5$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278423 1072 #uuid "33963d3c-06e1-501d-a9b3-58058a25683b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278423 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2y-x-5$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-5,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=5.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2y-x-5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,2y-x-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2y-x+2y-x-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[y]_{-1}^{0}\\cdot [{x}^{2}+5x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(0+1)\\cdot (1+5-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277879 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278424 1069 "q-161195" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278424 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-2y+1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 0$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278424 1072 #uuid "2844b31e-b68d-529c-a798-cb7912cb62d5" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278424 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x-2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,-2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x-2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,2x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}+1+{x}^{2}+1+2x-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[({y}^{2}-2y+1)+({x}^{2}+2x+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+1)^{2}\\cdot y+\\frac{(y-1)^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277953 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278425 1069 "q-161196" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278425 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278425 1072 #uuid "1edd1ea3-0f5b-55ca-9bd6-402b49560b3e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278425 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277986 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278426 1069 "q-161199" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278426 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278426 1072 #uuid "e0855a1e-4e9d-5aec-ac2d-5bafc7bc5253" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278426 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3(1-{e}^{z})+xy-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-3{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-3{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}{∬}_{S}xy-3{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277938 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278427 1069 "q-161200" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278427 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278427 1072 #uuid "fe4ccf73-c8f1-5e87-8e15-f27def60e911" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278427 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(xy-yz+2xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}xy-yz+2xz\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277997 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278428 1069 "q-161203" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278428 1071 "![](images/ce/cece4e226becb315d6d951a8ae782750da79209d9a67ec52a8cd4d134999e9e6.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278428 1072 #uuid "a533e788-011b-5fa3-9d77-cd8a43ced2a9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278428 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/21/21e29d99d2d5330db2d946d981e4bd0f820ce26297bf95f962cb3000f0998136.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}(4-{r}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[2{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot (18-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-18\\pi +0 \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278052 13