_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-{v}^{2}u]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{v}{3}-\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278172 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278432 1069 "q-161210" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278432 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278432 1072 #uuid "b8c858e2-999d-5a0b-bd3b-7bd241711034" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278432 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\cdot ⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-z(x-z)-2xy}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-xz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{{z}^{2}-xz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277772 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278433 1069 "q-161212" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278433 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}2xz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=u+v,\\,z=2v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278433 1072 #uuid "aaa6b101-413b-5c8f-a474-bfd4f4726f2b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278433 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =2xz\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =2u(2v) \\\\  & =4uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2xz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278006 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278434 1069 "q-161213" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278434 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by $z=y,$ $y=1,$ and the $y$-axis." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278434 1072 #uuid "1d826d14-2298-585c-90fc-f27a43f9e900" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278434 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cf571155f2a4cd78d83c4808326cd8102badb3c22f43e00d95aee2494859f48.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z)=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z)=\\sqrt{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{y}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=2y\\,\\text{d}y.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({2}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278088 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278435 1069 "q-161214" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278435 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $x={e}^{y}-z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by $z={e}^{2y},$ $y=0,$ $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $y$-axis." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278435 1072 #uuid "c338dfd1-e7f8-59f7-ac67-821e4b45c353" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278435 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{2y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9c/9cae5b68f77c2d79df8a81d0421146ac2bbe618c6065a457c33c86caceeb1327.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}-z)={e}^{y}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y}-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{y})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{2y}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z={e}^{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u={e}^{2y}+2,$ we get $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ u & 3 & e+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{3}^{e+2}\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((e+2)^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278081 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278436 1069 "q-161215" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278436 1071 "![](images/53/534c80a8318ebfe21ba3f21f04ae145f999d6d83c34a402294c789bb2090e014.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278436 1072 #uuid "1daafe6b-326b-58b0-829c-25583c57b290" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278436 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/7f/7f44bc35687020594b0d26a2e25b9dbd2b8d75c964a90184626afad6afc1b1e8.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1)+0 \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277928 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278437 1069 "q-161219" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278437 1071 "![](images/9a/9afc7f79f4612ce579e82a6b3c5086922d14051978907bfeb5c133a3f99791c7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278437 1072 #uuid "df8892a4-0ff3-5c81-818d-9a1c55b02265" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278437 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/fa/fa4986b1de44fa4ccc75114e64abfd93d0de16618f2f1b29695d9115019b6e3e.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+8y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278114 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278438 1069 "q-161222" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278438 1071 "![](images/01/01902836fd1e91ac829f67a279103a9b3d5732d7b058f03c695b6733a42e673d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278438 1072 #uuid "8c3f4c19-765d-5833-904a-bf7538da3049" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278438 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/c0/c058465d6ca7855446711ebc44f6b23913ee786d2fd9bb32ae6c671964e1ad32.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}+(-\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277779 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278439 1069 "q-161225" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278439 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278439 1072 #uuid "d04b955c-6599-5f09-8a8d-748ca0ca3061" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278439 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278111 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278440 1069 "q-161227" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278440 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$\n- ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$\n- $a=0$ and $b=1$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278440 1072 #uuid "2955e9f2-2674-539d-b301-d0ca76c12950" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278440 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and *above* by $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{2{x}^{2}+2z-1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/86/868fceefed2a426feebe2a9199279826aaee935963fec8fa3761b17b97542c34.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=-1$ and bounded *above* by $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277999 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278441 1069 "q-161230" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278441 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(y,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{3}+{e}^{z}$ that lies inside the elliptical cylinder $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278441 1072 #uuid "1e867d73-e633-518f-b45d-26ad639d5221" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278441 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{3}+{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})=3{y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})={e}^{z},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}+({e}^{z})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277826 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278442 1069 "q-161233" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278442 1071 "![](images/6b/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278442 1072 #uuid "87215c50-3d7b-51ff-bbb4-0e6c4fa9c71c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278442 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(x(x-y)+y(y+x)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =108\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277872 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278443 1069 "q-161235" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278443 1071 "![](images/c8/c8c7f186ba8769231e9245e0887501f666e56c317fd4f546550202956365b5dc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278443 1072 #uuid "b6a9b04d-4452-53ab-bc15-c881e40cd0e9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278443 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x(x+yz)+y(y-xz)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}({x}^{2}+xyz+{y}^{2}-xyz+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277973 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278444 1069 "q-161236" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278444 1071 "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y-{z}^{2},\\,z(1+y)⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278444 1072 #uuid "f51cefd5-2c4b-5f4a-83eb-ec5cac09a93e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278444 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y-{z}^{2},\\,z(1+y)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y(y-{z}^{2})+{z}^{2}(1+y) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+{z}^{2}+y{z}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277933 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278445 1069 "q-161237" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278445 1071 "![](images/1e/1ee9e32dc5dbe7ba1222a5448ce9ae1227a461a40a36f13e337fce7990263408.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $4$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278445 1072 #uuid "db773492-919e-574b-8b09-824e37e7dd1c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278445 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{4}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(xz+0-xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277945 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278446 1069 "q-161238" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278446 1071 "![](images/5f/5f393842a0e53fcacf439250245935fe7a7ad068973ee5f896f11916c3881e57.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278446 1072 #uuid "51769f9f-1b78-599f-9490-a10aeb86b399" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278446 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(x(x+xz)+{y}^{2}+z(z-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}({x}^{2}+{x}^{2}z+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\  & =20\\pi \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277962 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278447 1069 "q-161241" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278447 1071 "![](images/6b/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278447 1072 #uuid "c8f1bee6-220a-53d7-9c95-ba6fb2fa5c32" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278447 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278132 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278448 1069 "q-161242" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278448 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-3v,\\,z=4v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278448 1072 #uuid "81e32a26-4f8e-507c-9b65-da0df62a940b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278448 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u,\\,-3v,\\,4v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-3,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-8,\\,-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-8,\\,-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =x(y+z)\\,{|}_{x=2u\\,y=-3v\\,z=4v} \\\\  & =2u(-3v+4v) \\\\  & =2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2uv\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =5\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277834 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278449 1069 "q-161245" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278449 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=2u+v,\\,y=2u-2v,\\,z=u+2v\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $1\\le v\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278449 1072 #uuid "c63ddeef-33ef-5328-bfb9-278493d60c61" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278449 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,2u-2v,\\,u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-3,\\,-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\,-3,\\,-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u-2v\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =(2u+v)^{2}-(u+2v)^{2} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{∬}_{D}\\,(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{u}^{3}-3{v}^{2}u]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-6{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\cdot [2v-2{v}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277808 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278450 1069 "q-161247" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278450 1071 "![](images/60/60f622b161dbfb4da1d18e85d3eb3ab4e02c2af418f9845e05a3bf9704a28006.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$, $x=0,$ and $x=2+y-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2-y-z$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=2+y-z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=5$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278450 1072 #uuid "83e332b2-7a45-565f-946e-09fe8f179dcb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278450 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=2+y-z.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2af4029bd979d250ed15f351ef7f14d3ae688806db4ffec199a24b3591dadac.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277769 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278451 1069 "q-161248" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278451 1071 "![](images/40/40091bf7a66577a4a3f3b8cdf3b5da34cbe0e0198fdc5731c6d3dae8cb727418.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $z=0$, $z=3$, $y=0,$ and $y=4-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=4-z$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278451 1072 #uuid "8d0077c3-5809-5281-a04f-def492b7900c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278451 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1046e312a3783dfc3253646f8a71ae226a173ac882a6305850bdd81aed9dd7e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278024 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278452 1069 "q-161249" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278452 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,-uv,\\,{v}^{2}⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-4uv,\\,-2{u}^{2}⟩.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278452 1072 #uuid "a99addda-c910-54da-a8cb-ae3a5ce916a4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278452 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-4uv,\\,-2{u}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,z⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-uv\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-4uv,\\,-2{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+(-2uv)\\cdot (-4uv)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+8{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278080 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278453 1069 "q-161250" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278453 1071 "![](images/d4/d447e5a00aa324d83a3f513db11ff677fd2db2c7b1b33d8bc2bcfa73b4a08f3f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$, $y=0,$ and $y=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=z-\\frac{x}{3}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278453 1072 #uuid "9bbc7b8c-0af0-5315-ba58-db2c9cd43206" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278453 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$\n\n![](images/88/88b5fde383f094436dd94adefa7912f1a194319739fdb0b8a6a9a74693362219.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277955 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278454 1069 "q-161260" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278454 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\,x+z,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,2,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,0⟩.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278454 1072 #uuid "24885575-8fa2-5424-9a08-3593cc23273c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278454 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨y+z,\\,x+z,\\,1⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\,u-v+{v}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\,u-v+{v}^{2},\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(2+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+(u-v+{v}^{2})\\cdot (-2v)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\,x+z,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-{u}^{2}v+2u{v}^{2}-2u{v}^{3}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-v+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277963 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278455 1069 "q-161262" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278455 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\,x+y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3u{v}^{2},\\,u-v,\\,2⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278455 1072 #uuid "627dbfaa-1fc7-5620-9101-9c85744bbe99" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278455 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨{x}^{2},\\,x+y,\\,z⟩|}_{x=3u{v}^{2}\\,y=u-v\\,z=2} \\\\  & =⟨{(3u{v}^{2})}^{2},\\,3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\,3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\,3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =9{u}^{2}{v}^{4}\\cdot 0+(3u{v}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-6uv-3{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\,x+y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(u+\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}u]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-6(-2+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277787 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278456 1069 "q-161263" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278456 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278456 1072 #uuid "c5de597f-04b0-56f5-aa9e-d79ce26e7706" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278456 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3,\\,z,\\,y⟩|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,uv,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3,\\,uv,\\,v⟩\\cdot ⟨-v,\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-v)+uv\\cdot (-2{u}^{2})+v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-3uv-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{4}v+{u}^{2}v]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[-3{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278039 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278457 1069 "q-161268" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278457 1071 "${\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{2x{e}^{y}}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278457 1072 #uuid "22fb2249-99c2-54d5-98cc-ca6b4c445359" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278457 1074 "Since the domain of integration $R=[-1,2]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]\\times [-1,-\\frac{1}{2}]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{2x{e}^{y}}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}2x\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,dy{\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2x\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,dy{\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,dz & =[{x}^{2}]_{-1}^{2}\\cdot [{e}^{y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{z}]}_{-1}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =(4-1)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277989 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278458 1069 "q-161269" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278458 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278458 1072 #uuid "4285dbb3-527a-524c-bd67-eacc9dbedfd1" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278458 1074 "Since the domain of integration $R=[0,4]\\times [1,2]\\times [0,1]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,dx{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{3}\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,dx{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{3}\\,dz & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278163 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278459 1069 "q-161270" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278459 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278459 1072 #uuid "44ff0211-30a5-53e4-9da3-c64535815d63" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278459 1074 "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,4]\\times [1,2]\\times [0,1]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-0)(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{7}{3})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z & =(4-0)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{14}{3}-2=\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278140 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278460 1069 "q-161271" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278460 1071 "${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-4{y}^{3}-2z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278460 1072 #uuid "cedeaa7b-409d-5915-882a-6317bf3953a6" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278460 1074 "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,1]\\times [0,2]\\times [-1,1]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & =[{x}^{3}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & =(1-0)\\cdot [{y}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}z & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot [{z}^{2}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-4{y}^{3}-2z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=4-32-0=-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277749 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278461 1069 "q-161273" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278461 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,v-u,\\,-2uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278461 1072 #uuid "75e371a5-e04a-5fb9-938c-f538e06e2f93" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278461 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2v \\\\ 1 & -2u\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & -2u\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u+2v,\\,2u+2v,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\,0,\\,y⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-2uv} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\,0,\\,v-u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u-v,\\,0,\\,v-u⟩\\cdot ⟨2u+2v,\\,2u+2v,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (2u+2v)+0\\cdot (2u+2v)+(v-u)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}-u{v}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{3}v-\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278182 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278462 1069 "q-161274" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278462 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{y{e}^{z}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278462 1072 #uuid "0572eee0-823f-5798-8cce-9c697b0e5dd8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278462 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y{e}^{z}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{y{e}^{z}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]\\times [0,1]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{y{e}^{z}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot y\\cdot {e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & ={[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277819 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278463 1069 "q-161276" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278463 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{e}^{2}\\le y\\le {e}^{5},\\,0\\le z\\le 4}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278463 1072 #uuid "b297dd92-b10c-5ab9-9d26-be624175b717" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278463 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]\\times [{e}^{2},{e}^{5}]\\times [0,4]$ is a rectangular box, and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z & =[\\operatorname{tan}⁡x]_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}+1)\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}+1)\\cdot 3\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =24(\\sqrt{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277830 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278464 1069 "q-161277" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278464 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{z}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278464 1072 #uuid "0b1f4159-8e7b-5618-9563-62b772133333" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278464 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{z}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\times [-1,1]\\times [1,e]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =[{e}^{x}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot [y]_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡z]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(3-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278076 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278465 1069 "q-161278" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278465 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\,x-y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 0$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278465 1072 #uuid "5f5f7b83-0f5c-511f-9e98-a4ae85732cc3" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278465 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨-x-z,\\,x-y,\\,-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨-(u+v)-(-v),\\,u+v-(v-u),\\,-(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-u,\\,2u,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-u,\\,2u,\\,v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-u)\\cdot 1+2u\\cdot 1+v\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-u+2u+2v \\\\  & =u+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\,x-y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(u+2v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u+2v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[uv+{v}^{2}]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(u+1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+u]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277917 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278466 1069 "q-161279" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278466 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,5\\le z\\le 7}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278466 1072 #uuid "1f68fe38-9a38-5a58-9b2a-e9d32b22ab67" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278466 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [0,1]\\times [5,7]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}z & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{3}^{y-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-4)]_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =(-0+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277778 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278467 1069 "q-161283" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278467 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u{v}^{2},\\,-1,\\,1-2u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278467 1072 #uuid "d4391484-a70c-5d5d-8147-34baf59f21fd" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278467 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2uv,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {v}^{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-4uv,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩|}_{x=u{v}^{2}\\,y=-1\\,z=1-2u} \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,-3(u{v}^{2})(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,3u{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,3u{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-4uv,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(1-2u)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-4uv)+3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-8uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v\\, \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{1}{2})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278117 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278468 1069 "q-161285" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278468 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨-u,\\,-u,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278468 1072 #uuid "d149ed70-6a6c-529d-a7c4-ab22e040de29" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278468 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y)\\,{|}_{x=-u\\,y=-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =(-u)^{2}\\cdot (-v)^{2}-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277914 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278469 1069 "q-161286" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278469 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\,u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278469 1072 #uuid "d9e295ad-a9d0-5267-be9d-f4d887cdb7cb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278469 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-2u & 1 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2u \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,0,\\,2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2v,\\,0,\\,2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2v)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-x-y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =-u-({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277739 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278470 1069 "q-161290" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278470 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x-{y}^{2}z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨uv,\\,-u,\\,-{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278470 1072 #uuid "616b2956-33ca-5451-b2d1-058d45da4207" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278470 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ v & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 0 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}v & 0 \\\\ u & -2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}v & -1 \\\\ u & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,2{v}^{2},\\,u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\,2{v}^{2},\\,u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(2{v}^{2})^{2}+(u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x-{y}^{2}z)\\,{|}_{x=uv\\,y=-u\\,z=-{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv-(-u)^{2}\\cdot -{v}^{2} \\\\  & =uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278094 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278471 1069 "q-161291" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278471 1071 "![](images/97/9795c04bf6d4bfa407a7b9176cd5c4b97b3d89bfcc6fff17d1d78190047b3a54.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278471 1072 #uuid "5610a24a-7851-5e97-8ed8-8bbb2532b7e3" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278471 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277897 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278472 1069 "q-161295" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278472 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{e}{e}^{-z}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278472 1072 #uuid "73e0bb40-7924-5746-8ab3-84c1b2f65060" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278472 1074 "Since the domain of integration $R=[0,2]\\times [0,e]\\times [0,1]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{e}{e}^{-z}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}dx{\\int }_{0}^{e}dy{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-z}\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}dx{\\int }_{0}^{e}dy{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-z}\\,dz & =(2-0)\\cdot (e-0)\\cdot [-{e}^{-z}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot e\\cdot (-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2e(1-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277832 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278473 1069 "q-161299" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278473 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278473 1072 #uuid "b4fa809c-90f5-5143-bcfa-9f2ac7d10fda" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278473 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi }\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+0))\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}(-0+1)\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,dx & =-2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277858 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278474 1069 "q-161300" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278474 1071 "![](images/01/0148ca16ee12a0f651c91091715b4ec07d95639530defb8ed366bacb33e4e87d.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278474 1072 #uuid "86501c09-fb5b-5eb4-8dc1-b7ee80036bf5" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278474 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}(1+1) \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277853 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278475 1069 "q-161301" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278475 1071 "![](images/52/5276afad13c3e52854d33a3ff3404ab401781e08d1888214121cf2d56c0bb304.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+2y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278475 1072 #uuid "7e7a3fcc-a15c-5e4a-92b5-d91d52ab682a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278475 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32(1+1) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278135 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278476 1069 "q-161303" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278476 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,{u}^{2}+2v,\\,{u}^{3}+3v⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{u}^{2}+bu+c\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278476 1072 #uuid "a2d1066e-0b79-5532-9ae3-4e710fbc5bc4" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278476 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2u,\\,3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2u \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}-3,\\,2-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩{|}_{x=u+v\\,y={u}^{2}+2v\\,z={u}^{3}+3v} \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}-3,\\,2-2u⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (6u-6{u}^{2})+0\\cdot (3{u}^{2}-3)+2\\cdot (2-2u) \\\\  & =6u-6{u}^{2}+4-4u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{2}+2u+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6)\\cdot {u}^{2}+2\\cdot u+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+2+4=0.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278057 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278477 1069 "q-161306" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278477 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\,y+\\frac{1}{z},\\,z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u{e}^{v},\\,u{e}^{-v},\\,u⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{e}^{v}+b{e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278477 1072 #uuid "8ba2bc0a-f01f-56b1-8fa8-5c3da0d50d17" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278477 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{-v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{v},\\,{e}^{-v},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{-v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v},\\,-u{e}^{-v},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v}\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{-v},\\,u{e}^{v},\\,-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\,y+\\frac{1}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{x=u{e}^{v}\\,y=u{e}^{-v}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\,u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\,u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\,u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\,u⟩\\cdot ⟨u{e}^{-v},\\,u{e}^{v},\\,-2u⟩ \\\\  & =(u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{-v}+(u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{v}+u\\cdot (-2u) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{e}^{-v}+{u}^{2}+{e}^{v}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{v}-{e}^{-v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot {e}^{v}+(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1-1=0.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277860 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278478 1069 "q-161307" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278478 1071 "${\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{y}^{2}-4x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278478 1072 #uuid "84e92597-f73b-5d66-82f1-bd7ed253a110" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278478 1074 "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,1]\\times [1,2]\\times [2,5]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}z & =(1-0)\\cdot [{y}^{3}]_{1}^{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (8-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}z & =[2{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z=21-6=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277783 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278479 1069 "q-161308" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278479 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{y}{z})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278479 1072 #uuid "8ad96b4f-2e89-5c16-badd-aa8debecf095" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278479 1074 "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,2]\\times [1,e]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\text{d}z & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi }\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =(1+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =4(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =(\\pi -0)\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{y}{z})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=4(e-1)+2\\pi =2(2e+\\pi -2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278144 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278480 1069 "q-161310" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278480 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{z}({y}^{2}-x)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278480 1072 #uuid "9deafc4d-48f6-5fbe-8732-c2d81669b17a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278480 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{z}({y}^{2}-x)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{z}{y}^{2}-{e}^{z}x\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{z}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}-x{e}^{z}[y]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{z}(9-0)-x{e}^{z}(3-0)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}9{e}^{z}-3x{e}^{z}\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}9{e}^{z}-3x{e}^{z}\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{z}(9-3x)\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(9-3x)[{e}^{z}]_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(9-3x)(2-1)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}9-3x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}9-3x\\,dx & ={[9x-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(18-6)-(9-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277840 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278481 1069 "q-161311" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278481 1071 "![](images/5b/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278481 1072 #uuid "cc8526c3-3b66-50ba-a72f-6803fda09a5e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278481 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277862 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278482 1069 "q-161313" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278482 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-y)^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278482 1072 #uuid "b33311e2-1291-593c-88fe-67f6c6bcae07" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278482 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-y)^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-y)^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{(x-y)^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z[x]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-y)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(0-y)^{3}}{3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z(1-0)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-y)^{3}+{y}^{3}}{3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-y)^{3}+{y}^{3}}{3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-y)^{3}+{y}^{3}}{3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{(1-y)^{3}+{y}^{3}}{3}[z]_{z=0}^{z=2\\pi }+[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=2\\pi })\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{(1-y)^{3}+{y}^{3}}{3}(2\\pi -0)+(-1+1))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-y)^{3}+{y}^{3}\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-y)^{3}+{y}^{3}\\text{d}y & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{[-\\frac{(1-y)^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}((-0+\\frac{1}{4})-(-\\frac{1}{4}+0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278053 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278483 1069 "q-161318" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278483 1071 "![](images/5d/5d6cedcc2d8c5a232d8d7ed89f050fa7a545ed0da6075ddb44eec67e065b9e87.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278483 1072 #uuid "23220eb4-f0ea-5f75-a067-2a7409386baf" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278483 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=-4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277886 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278484 1069 "q-161319" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278484 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278484 1072 #uuid "e62de12f-3877-52f7-8f85-f37913bbcbfa" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278484 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{6}\\,xy(y-2x)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277742 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278485 1069 "q-161321" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278485 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}xyz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278485 1072 #uuid "71274639-1883-57e5-9cbd-1e009801625d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278485 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}xy(-\\sqrt{2}x-\\sqrt{2}y)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-2{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2})\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,-2\\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278157 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278486 1069 "q-161322" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278486 1071 "![](images/92/9215246969b621002a96dec3aed8a012045b8061371e4c6a1234f54d53de29cc.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{3}y$ whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278486 1072 #uuid "cedd414d-7a92-5f38-8d1c-98c03492493d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278486 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277902 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278487 1069 "q-161325" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278487 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278487 1072 #uuid "26332fd2-3603-5c3f-9cd0-0420c8d9d4db" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278487 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(4y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,({x}^{6}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{4}+4{y}^{4}{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278113 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278488 1069 "q-161330" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278488 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278488 1072 #uuid "d00a0fdd-b39f-55a4-9c73-ceedda035fbc" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278488 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dx]dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y+z)]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+y+z)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+y+z)\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+z)\\,dy\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+z)\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+z)\\,dy]dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+z)]}_{y=0}^{y=z}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(z+z)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2z+z)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+z)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+z)\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3z-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3z-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2z+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3z+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z]}_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]-[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+0+0]-[-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277932 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278489 1069 "q-161333" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278489 1071 "$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nGiven that the triple integral above is considered over the rectangular box\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278489 1072 #uuid "7f6bae96-335a-57c2-b27f-061a38c2dd92" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278489 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=1+4=5.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277965 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278490 1069 "q-161334" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278490 1071 "$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz\n$$\n\nGiven that the triple integral above is considered over the rectangular box\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le e,\\,1\\le z\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278490 1072 #uuid "394bd36d-1f37-5a02-b559-18a3a440143f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278490 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{e}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=-2+3=1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278123 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278491 1069 "q-161336" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278491 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}z}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278491 1072 #uuid "cb39b773-6ddf-5c8b-9d03-9af33b6087cf" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278491 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}z}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}z}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{[z\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{3}z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{3}z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{3}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot y\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,y\\,dy & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278101 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278492 1069 "q-161339" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278492 1071 "![](images/d1/d19d54d0e76ced24d1bd9f865578b72f2b8d67414c8506499c6ae7b1300a6275.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-2x+2y$ and whose domain is the left half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278492 1072 #uuid "e935e93c-c375-5e87-b42b-951f2a795ef1" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278492 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+2y)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278161 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278493 1069 "q-161340" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278493 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-2\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278493 1072 #uuid "5e393098-f8db-5718-a78a-3c0ee5f6534f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278493 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2xy}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}}\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(2xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{2}]_{y=-2}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277855 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278494 1069 "q-161341" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278494 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278494 1072 #uuid "b54f01b4-5da4-54d3-8529-0362ccff055b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278494 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4(x+y)\\,\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4xy+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[2{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278121 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278495 1069 "q-161344" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278495 1071 "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\frac{x}{z}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278495 1072 #uuid "6a641492-289d-52ec-861c-68bcfc571351" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278495 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\frac{x}{z}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\frac{x}{z}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{x}{z}\\cdot z\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})]}_{y=0}^{y={z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{z})]}_{y=0}^{y={z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x(-0+1)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\,dx & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=-1}^{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277954 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278496 1069 "q-161347" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278496 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le e.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278496 1072 #uuid "48f0648b-6535-5240-b098-b0f79d4f5777" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278496 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{(2x+y)-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{y=1}^{y=e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277951 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278497 1069 "q-161348" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278497 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=5x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2x.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278497 1072 #uuid "4c18475c-c837-5db2-8109-d064b9976225" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278497 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x-y)=5,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+(5x-y)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\sqrt{27}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,(26{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}(26{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[26{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-5x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(52{x}^{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-20{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{104{x}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}104{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[26{x}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =26\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277805 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278498 1069 "q-161352" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278498 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{36x}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+8y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278498 1072 #uuid "0921ef67-5139-5147-a7ee-44b4f995ddab" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278498 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+8y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+8y)=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{36x}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4x+8y}{36x}\\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,9\\frac{4(x+2y)}{36x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(1+\\frac{2y}{x})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[y+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x}-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277929 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278499 1069 "q-161353" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278499 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278499 1072 #uuid "70e559da-f697-5786-a70f-285bfb1e201a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278499 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}(1+x+y)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}\\,\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(-\\frac{1}{1+x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+x]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278097 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278500 1069 "q-161356" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278500 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V,\\,\n$$\n\nover the box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le z\\le 1}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278500 1072 #uuid "302c1f3a-6644-51f1-b377-2ef8ff41ad4d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278500 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [\\frac{1}{2},1]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi }\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{z}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+1)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (-1+2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277971 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278501 1069 "q-161358" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278501 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=3x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 5$ and $-3\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278501 1072 #uuid "0bf481ef-95d3-5b3f-b324-29ecd57febda" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278501 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,-3\\le v\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=3u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-2v)\\cdot (-3)+(-v)\\cdot (2)+2u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+6v-2v+2u \\\\  & =-7u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278116 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278502 1069 "q-161361" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278502 1071 "![](images/f7/f7799ca5801bf019fc5b884590575346a8f298f8e9c91056c13a60c4dd23742d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=3$, $x=0,$ and $x=4-y+2z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=4+y-2z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278502 1072 #uuid "59f1a985-1f99-5d8b-a66e-0643cac8da20" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278502 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true and statement III is false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bf8e141067fb6f675933f52e465114d8909d951f6ac85fb09c8dda7efbc2f38.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277813 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278503 1069 "q-161362" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278503 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{{y}^{2}(z-1)}{x}\\,\\text{d}V,\\,\n$$\n\nover the box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le z\\le 3}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278503 1072 #uuid "68142e08-63c3-593e-9809-ce4eb5387cbb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278503 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{{y}^{2}(z-1)}{x}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{y}^{2}(z-1)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[1,e]\\times [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{y}^{2}(z-1)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (z-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{3}(z-1)\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{3}(z-1)\\,\\text{d}z & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{1}^{e}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}-z]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-0)\\cdot (\\frac{9}{2}-3-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278164 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278504 1069 "q-161366" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278504 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+z\\,dV\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278504 1072 #uuid "152ed04a-265c-588d-9a68-27d973d01a48" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278504 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+z\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nSince we're integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z,$ we write $\\text{d}V=\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+z\\,dx]dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+xz]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(1-0)z\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+z\\,dy\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+z\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+z\\,dy]dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+yz]}_{y=0}^{y=\\pi }\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}(0-0)+(\\pi -0)z\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\pi z\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\pi z\\,dz & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,dz \\\\  & =\\pi {[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277908 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278505 1069 "q-161367" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278505 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\,dV\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278505 1072 #uuid "b11ea46a-280b-5c15-a86e-319f41628afb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278505 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy\n$$\n\nSince we're integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ we write $\\text{d}V=\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x{y}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi }\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}-z(-1-1)+{y}^{2}(\\pi -0)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2z+\\pi {y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2z+\\pi {y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2z+\\pi {y}^{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{z}^{2}+\\pi {y}^{2}z]_{z=-1}^{z=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-1)+\\pi {y}^{2}(2-(-1))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3+3\\pi {y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3+3\\pi {y}^{2}\\,dy & =[3y+\\pi {y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =3(1-0)+\\pi (1-0) \\\\  & =3+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278154 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278506 1069 "q-161368" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278506 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z{e}^{x}+2y\\,dV\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278506 1072 #uuid "3d986402-4e78-5488-8c0a-1084c6fc07c8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278506 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z{e}^{x}+2y\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}z{e}^{x}+2y\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy\n$$\n\nSince we're integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ we write $\\text{d}V=\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}z{e}^{x}+2y\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}z{e}^{x}+2y\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z{e}^{x}+2xy]_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}z({e}^{3}-1)+2(3-0)y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{3}-1)z+6y\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{3}-1)z+6y\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{3}-1)z+6y\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{2}\\,{z}^{2}+6yz]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{2}(1-0)+6y(1-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{2}+6y\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{2}+6y\\,dy & ={[\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{2}\\,y+3{y}^{2}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{2}(1-(-1))+3(1-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277792 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278507 1069 "q-161369" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278507 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ and then $z,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}z+\\frac{1}{y}\\,dV\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-1\\le z\\le 1}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278507 1072 #uuid "a129d5b8-378f-5b2a-be41-9e6ab835f2b0" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278507 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}z+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}z+\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz\n$$\n\nSince we're integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ and then $z,$ we write $\\text{d}V=\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}z+\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}z+\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy]dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}zy+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|]_{y=1}^{y=3}\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}z(3-1)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-0)\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}z+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,dx\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}z+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,dx\\,dz & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}2z{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,dx]dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}\\,z{x}^{3}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}\\,z(8-0)+(2-0)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{16}{3}\\,z+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{16}{3}z+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,dz & ={[\\frac{8}{3}{z}^{2}+2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]}_{z=-1}^{z=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1-1)+2(1-(-1))\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277898 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278508 1069 "q-161371" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278508 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dV\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\pi }.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278508 1072 #uuid "19e5847f-a29d-5d8b-9d46-19900df81c90" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278508 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nSince we're integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x,$ we write $\\text{d}V=\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y+z)]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+z+\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+z+0)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+z)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+z)\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+z)\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+z)\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+z)]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi }\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx & =4[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(0-1) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278032 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278509 1069 "q-161372" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278509 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}5x-3y\\,dV\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-1\\le z\\le 0}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278509 1072 #uuid "817c782f-a943-5a46-a15f-10d5dc0fa718" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278509 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}5x-3y\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}5x-3y\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nSince we're integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x,$ we write $\\text{d}V=\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}5x-3y\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}5x-3y\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[5xy-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}5x(2-0)-\\frac{3}{2}(4-0)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}10x-6\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}10x-6\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-1}^{0}10x-6\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(10x-6)z]_{z=-1}^{z=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(10x-6)(0-(-1))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10x-6\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}10x-6\\,dx & =[5{x}^{2}-6x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =5(1-0)-6(1-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278179 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278510 1069 "q-161373" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278510 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x+3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}\\,dV\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278510 1072 #uuid "cda1ab64-753e-5e60-b1d5-120d52bd3157" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278510 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x+3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x+3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nSince we're integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z,$ we write $\\text{d}V=\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x+3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x+3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}\\,dx]dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}+(3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3})x]_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9-0)+(3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3})(3-0)\\,dy\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}9+9{y}^{2}+12{z}^{3}\\,dy\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}9+9{y}^{2}+12{z}^{3}\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}9+12{z}^{3}+9{y}^{2}\\,dy]dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(9+12{z}^{3})y+3{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9+12{z}^{3})(2-0)+3(8-0)\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}18+24{z}^{3}+24\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}42+24{z}^{3}\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}42+24{z}^{3}\\,dz & =[42z+6{z}^{4}]_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =42(1-0)+6(1-0) \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278007 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278511 1069 "q-161380" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278511 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $x=\\sqrt{2y}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278511 1072 #uuid "62e7d4a5-ede7-555e-89aa-1ccf67e4530f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278511 1074 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98b9dc84e3d23d43c1fef204109d6f6b6c571d6f188592f1f08bb1abf4dc3641.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, and statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the paraboloid is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the cone, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278137 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278512 1069 "q-161382" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278512 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278512 1072 #uuid "154ea74b-7c8a-5b64-880d-29244a657638" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278512 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xy(2x+y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{-x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{xy(2x+y)}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 2-(-y)\\cdot (1)+xy(2x+y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+\\frac{1}{2}+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277777 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278513 1069 "q-161384" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278513 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,x⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,u⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩.$*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278513 1072 #uuid "92e0422d-8551-525d-a92c-4ab1ea25fb60" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278513 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,x⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278082 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278514 1069 "q-161386" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278514 1071 "The region $R$ is bounded between  the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\frac{1}{4}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$\n- $a=-3$ and $b=3$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278514 1072 #uuid "0a480e12-228f-5db7-81b6-a3f28cbc1181" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278514 1074 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and *above* by $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/46/46757c2bf93af4916afbd019f0faed2eef25245e987acbeff7eafb3e0ca7cd8c.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{3}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278056 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278515 1069 "q-161435" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278515 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278515 1072 #uuid "d5fb8cb6-33d1-582b-8cf3-53ceebac82e8" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278515 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=x+y} \\\\  & =⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{xy}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{x+y}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\,x+y,\\,{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(xy)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot 1+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{x+y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-e)-(0+\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2}-8e-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277949 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278516 1069 "q-161441" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278516 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278516 1072 #uuid "279f5fbf-fc31-5667-8b25-7d1c25dc2d02" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278516 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,v-(u-2v),\\,u+v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,3v-u,\\,u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,3v-u,\\,u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(3v-u)\\cdot 2+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u+6v-2u+u+v \\\\  & =-2u+7v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2u+7v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2u+7v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277882 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278517 1069 "q-161443" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278517 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\frac{1}{xz}$ that lies inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278517 1072 #uuid "1e50892b-6dbf-5e7c-ae2e-f87a4a349f42" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278517 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{xz},\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}{z}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277795 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278518 1069 "q-161446" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278518 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278518 1072 #uuid "1aa60ad7-eedc-5a6c-9adc-f826e8cc15e1" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278518 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(-Q\\,)\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277972 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278519 1069 "q-161450" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278519 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}6{y}^{2}\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type I region given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x,\\,0\\le z\\le x+y}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278519 1072 #uuid "9ad4979e-99f7-5f66-a14c-bdd27693322d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278519 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}6{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{x+y}6{y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{x+y}6{y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[{\\int }_{0}^{x+y}6{y}^{2}\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[6{y}^{2}z]_{z=0}^{z=x+y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}6{y}^{2}(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}6x{y}^{2}+6{y}^{3}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}6x{y}^{2}+6{y}^{3}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}6x{y}^{2}+6{y}^{3}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2x{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{4}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{4}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{4}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{7}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278151 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278520 1069 "q-161451" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278520 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}24(z-1)\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type I region given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x,\\,0\\le z\\le 2x-y}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278520 1072 #uuid "39370d41-8a1d-5ac9-ab0e-059b0fb3af29" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278520 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}24(z-1)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{2x-y}24(z-1)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{2x-y}24(z-1)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[{\\int }_{0}^{2x-y}24(z-1)\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[12{z}^{2}-24z]_{z=0}^{z=2x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(12(2x-y)^{2}-24(2x-y))\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}12(4{x}^{2}-4xy+{y}^{2}-4x+2y)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}12(4{x}^{2}-4xy+{y}^{2}-4x+2y)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}48({x}^{2}-x)+24(1-2x)y+12{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[48({x}^{2}-x)y+12(1-2x){y}^{2}+4{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}48({x}^{2}-x)x+12(1-2x){x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}28{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}28{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}\\,dx & ={[7{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(112-96)-(7-12) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277811 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278521 1069 "q-161453" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278521 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $y=-{x}^{2}z$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\,xz+Q+\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278521 1072 #uuid "ce5730b3-fc65-5b4c-bdd5-95a34bf939c5" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278521 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=-{x}^{2}z,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,-{x}^{2}z,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,-2xz,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,-{x}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2xz & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨-2xz,\\,-1,\\,-{x}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2P\\,xz+Q+R\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277896 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278522 1069 "q-161456" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278522 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=2yz$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\,z+\\,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278522 1072 #uuid "a6603224-9e78-54fd-8272-af20dda792ce" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278522 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=2yz,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨2yz,\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨2z,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨2y,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2z & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2y & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(y,z))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(\\,-2Q)\\,z+(\\,-2R)\\,y\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277984 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278523 1069 "q-161459" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278523 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2z\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type I region given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le x,\\,x\\le z\\le 1+x+y}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278523 1072 #uuid "65b0221a-16bf-5639-ba7a-c83e732f02f6" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278523 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}{\\int }_{x}^{1+x+y}2z\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}{\\int }_{x}^{1+x+y}2z\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}[{\\int }_{x}^{1+x+y}2z\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}[{z}^{2}]_{z=x}^{z=1+x+y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}(1+x+y)^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}(1+y)^{2}+2x(1+y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}{y}^{2}+2xy+2y+2x+1\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}({y}^{2}+2xy+2y+2x+1)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{1}^{x}({y}^{2}+2xy+2y+2x+1)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2xy+y]}_{y=1}^{y=x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}+x)-(\\frac{1}{3}+x+1+2x+1)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{7}{3}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{7}{3}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{3}x]}_{x=1}^{x=3} \\\\  & =(27+27-9-7)-(\\frac{1}{3}+1-1-\\frac{7}{3}) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278085 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278524 1069 "q-161462" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278524 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278524 1072 #uuid "06f1e075-9f39-52f0-b9b8-c179d2533ffc" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278524 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,v-u⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=v-u} \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\,v+2(v-u),\\,2u-v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\,3v-2u,\\,2u-v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v-u,\\,3v-2u,\\,2u-v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(v-u)\\cdot 1+(3v-2u)\\cdot (-1)+(2u-v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u-3v+2u+2u-v \\\\  & =3u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3u-3v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(3u-3v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277828 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278525 1069 "q-161465" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278525 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P-\\,-R\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278525 1072 #uuid "46dbd1e0-5399-5e33-b950-1f08b8d717ef" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278525 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P-Q\\,\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\,\\frac{∂k}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278013 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278526 1069 "q-161468" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278526 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}z\\,dz]dA\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278526 1072 #uuid "e1b378b9-d205-5df7-bb13-89eafbe8b8c9" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278526 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}z\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}{[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}})}^{2}-0\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{2}(y-{x}^{2})\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{2}(y-{x}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}(y-{x}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}y-{x}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=4}dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(16-{x}^{4})-{x}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+8\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+8x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(32-0)-\\frac{2}{3}(8-0)+4(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{16}{3}+8 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277956 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278527 1069 "q-161469" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278527 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4{z}^{3}\\,dz]dA\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278527 1072 #uuid "4bb3055c-7389-561e-9743-c05ebddec059" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278527 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4{z}^{3}\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}{[{z}^{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({(\\sqrt{y})}^{4}-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1-x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}((1-x)^{3}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[-\\frac{1}{4}(1-x)^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277925 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278528 1069 "q-161481" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278528 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278528 1072 #uuid "da0b12bf-561c-5907-8348-62e84bca5b63" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278528 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨{{1}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277820 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278529 1069 "q-161483" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278529 1071 "Consider the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,-1\\le z\\le \\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $xy$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278529 1072 #uuid "5971a51a-2743-586c-b0c7-bdb5998e5c16" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278529 1074 "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=-1\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278178 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278530 1069 "q-161484" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278530 1071 "Consider the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,2-x-y\\le z\\le 4},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $xy$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278530 1072 #uuid "bb06e36b-6fd7-5765-9bec-ecfe4efb6c9d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278530 1074 "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=2-x-y\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2-x-y}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278070 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278531 1069 "q-161485" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278531 1071 "Consider the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,x+y-1\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+3},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $xy$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278531 1072 #uuid "1762ad0b-9867-5233-ba08-00097ae95458" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278531 1074 "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=x+y-1\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)={x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x+y-1}^{{x}^{2}+3}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278017 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278532 1069 "q-161488" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278532 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+y}}2z\\,dz]dA\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278532 1072 #uuid "dba4c3e0-a278-560a-9fc6-d1665226a858" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278532 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+y}}2z\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{x+y}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({(\\sqrt{x+y})}^{2}-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x+y\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x+y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}x+y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}x+y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[xy+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}-0)+\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{4}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0)+\\frac{1}{10}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278102 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278533 1069 "q-161489" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278533 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{y+1}\\frac{z}{4}\\,dz]dA\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 4x}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278533 1072 #uuid "03ab1967-2b2d-5f26-b80f-1cccdf59895f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278533 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{y+1}\\frac{z}{4}\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}{[\\frac{1}{8}{z}^{2}]}_{z=1}^{z=y+1}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{8}((y+1)^{2}-1)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{8}({y}^{2}+2y)\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{8}({y}^{2}+2y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}\\frac{1}{8}({y}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{4x}\\frac{1}{8}({y}^{2}+2y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4x}dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}(64{x}^{3}-0)+(16{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{64}{3}{x}^{3}+16{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{16}{3}{x}^{4}+\\frac{16}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-0)+\\frac{2}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277924 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278534 1069 "q-161490" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278534 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{x+y}(y+2z)\\,dz]dA\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278534 1072 #uuid "b96415a7-aa98-5152-97b4-92a162dd6822" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278534 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{x+y}(y+2z)\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[yz+{z}^{2}]_{z=x}^{z=x+y}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y((x+y)-x)+((x+y)^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x({x}^{2}-0)+\\frac{2}{3}({x}^{3}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}(16-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278180 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278535 1069 "q-161495" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278535 1071 "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+xy}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278535 1072 #uuid "a0242a03-0f03-514c-a8ce-c5b7bb6c7216" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278535 1074 "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)=1+xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=-2+4=2.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277758 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278536 1069 "q-161496" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278536 1071 "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}y\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278536 1072 #uuid "2d3dc183-94aa-5676-a2cc-9d6fdb8cb215" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278536 1074 "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}y\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+4=5.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277782 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278537 1069 "q-161498" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278537 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,x\\le z\\le 0},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xy$-plane enclosed between the coordinate axes and the line $y=2+2x$ in the second quadrant." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278537 1072 #uuid "b2550aa4-7ccc-5483-87f0-a69ce9722fdb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278537 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{0}2y\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{0}2y\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[2yz]_{z=x}^{z=0}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y(0-x)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2xy\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10a5bb8e61589b71634a21dd17c342eb3f8212995aef52e28f8e8e8033fcdc7d.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-2xy\\,dA & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2+2x}2xy\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{2+2x}2xy\\text{ }\\,dy]dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2+2x}dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}x{(2+2x)}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4x+8{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & =-{[2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}]}_{x=-1}^{x=0} \\\\  & =-0+(2-\\frac{8}{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277866 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278538 1069 "q-161500" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278538 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}16x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,0\\le z\\le x-y},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xy$-plane enclosed between the coordinate axes and the line $y=2x-3$ in the fourth quadrant." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278538 1072 #uuid "4169910a-ce7d-5d1b-9b0e-97a83be3a13e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278538 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}16x\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x-y}16x\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x-y}16x\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[16xz]_{z=0}^{z=x-y}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}16x(x-y)\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bddbd1c97fe8cd74f9903dec340495f04401323b8e1236680c57c13b18069eb4.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}16x(x-y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}{\\int }_{2x-3}^{0}16x(x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}16x[{\\int }_{2x-3}^{0}(x-y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}16x{[xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=2x-3}^{y=0}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}16x(0-x(2x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(2x-3)^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}8x(-4{x}^{2}+6x+4{x}^{2}-12x+9)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}8x(-6x+9)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}-48{x}^{2}+72x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-16{x}^{3}+36{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3/2} \\\\  & =-16{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}+36{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-54+81 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277856 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278539 1069 "q-161506" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278539 1071 "What is the value of $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t+2} \\\\ 5-{t}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-2)\\end{aligned}]$ at $t=2?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278539 1072 #uuid "b1dec48d-778a-532f-b451-c350f2619a0a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278539 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $t=2$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\ 5-{2}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4} \\\\ 5-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the value of $f(t)$ at $t=2$ is the vector $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277789 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278540 1069 "q-161507" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278540 1071 "What is the value of $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-4) \\\\ 5t \\\\ 1-2t\\end{aligned}]$ at $t=5?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278540 1072 #uuid "10894d9f-77c7-5720-8c2d-f5aa520bb510" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278540 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $t=5$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4) \\\\ 5(5) \\\\ 1-2(5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ {5}^{2} \\\\ 1-10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 25 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the value of $f(t)$ at $t=5$ is the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 25 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277870 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278541 1069 "q-161509" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278541 1071 "What is the value of $f(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t+1},\\,{t}^{2}-3,\\,1-t⟩$ at $t=1?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278541 1072 #uuid "e9e18ee2-a874-5ad9-a576-f430b483878c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278541 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $t=1$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{1+1},{1}^{2}-3,1-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2},1-3,1-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2},-2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $f(t)$ at $t=1$ is the vector $⟨\\sqrt{2},-2,0⟩.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277838 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278542 1069 "q-161510" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278542 1071 "What is the value of $f(t)=⟨\\frac{4}{t-1},\\,1+{t}^{3},\\,(t-1)^{4}⟩$ at $t=3?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278542 1072 #uuid "f3cd75d0-9b9c-56ab-b0bf-df2c562d6d74" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278542 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $t=3$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{3-1},1+{3}^{3},(3-1)^{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{2},1+27,{2}^{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,28,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $f(t)$ at $t=3$ is the vector $⟨2,28,16⟩.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278079 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278543 1069 "q-161511" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278543 1071 "What is the value of $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-3),\\,\\sqrt{2t},\\,{t}^{5}⟩$ at $t=2?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278543 1072 #uuid "f77f5ccf-c791-5450-abda-c69d732b7b8e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278543 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $t=2$ into the function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2}-3),\\sqrt{2(2)},{2}^{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1),\\sqrt{4},{2}^{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,32⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $f(t)$ at $t=2$ is the vector $⟨0,2,32⟩.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277864 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278544 1069 "q-161514" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278544 1071 "![](images/33/330b550b31987e29aceb4b2a253e26ebec470692a6a0144f64725801f2df2b7d.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}x\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above. \n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278544 1072 #uuid "d8703809-a278-5ca6-8ab6-1851ce509d1f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278544 1074 "![](images/03/03a91dfd9f6a69455157c47162b11cd69cb3b03cfabea2770b94f7322015b0e2.png)\n\n \nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+2y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac3eafe926b84588703bb8a56a1529ebe1352d25764a523b97c684a4f4e95201.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+2y}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1+2y}x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1+2y}x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+2y}x\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[xz]_{z=0}^{z=1+2y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(1+2y)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(1+2y)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(1+2y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y+{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6x\\,dx & =[3{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277847 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278545 1069 "q-161516" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278545 1071 "![](images/9d/9d6a1a5ca848d7563763a3384855daf13031c808852efa541a73e2ef3126c6ed.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}4xy\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above. \n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278545 1072 #uuid "32008f80-009f-566a-86ce-b133beef3e07" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278545 1074 "![](images/35/35113579a0c74805710c8787ba8738cd76c2777d59cf2a0cd234b284760ca6f6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x+y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/01/012a2a575147df3cf7fc0a5b85443d461c77c8447797969d8b96d9007cbfffd2.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x+y}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[4xyz]_{z=0}^{z=3-x+y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy(3-x+y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[6x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x)-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{22}{3}x-2{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{22}{3}x-2{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{11}{3}{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{11}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278086 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278546 1069 "q-161517" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278546 1071 "![](images/89/89fba52c9a48d4f8f2ddac0b060c3ffab7e3ba4f6652124f4a9ac6ba39a579e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}(3x-1)\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278546 1072 #uuid "97d0239a-f000-519b-82e3-2ce3c3f4f34b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278546 1074 "![](images/86/86c1bef04ebf536b10f3f7d61eafcff909bd439b7641cbfc31cecd05cca23c18.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-x+y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dfa0a80bc323401f050d1302dc42c92558d8b566d9b0f985e955d34eb5cc15bd.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-x+y}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(3x-1)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x+y}(3x-1)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x+y}(3x-1)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x+y}(3x-1)\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x-1)[z]_{z=0}^{z=2-x+y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x-1)(2-x+y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3xy-y-3{x}^{2}+7x-2\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3xy-y-3{x}^{2}+7x-2\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x-1)y+(-3{x}^{2}+7x-2)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{3x-1}{2}\\,{y}^{2}+(-3{x}^{2}+7x-2)y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2(3x-1)+2(-3{x}^{2}+7x-2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-6{x}^{2}+20x-6\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-6{x}^{2}+20x-6\\,dx & =[-2{x}^{3}+10x-6x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(-2+10-6)-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278160 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278547 1069 "q-161518" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278547 1071 "![](images/00/00e09ca44feb1fcfa5d3ece010a858dbd98efe57985b0f8d5210f9d98a02f498.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278547 1072 #uuid "0827499d-6c2c-5ac7-9fa8-3ecadfbbe8c2" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278547 1074 "![](images/90/9002d6f1e55a734e410fc85111816294ed037c4a4139e9bc8bceb53c62daeeb6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+x},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31f2ff96afa1248427c1a6dd5074c47190c70983d7c0d5dfdd3def8bd883aef8.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+x}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}{y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}{y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}{y}^{2}\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{y}^{2}z]_{z=0}^{z=1+x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(1+x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(1+x)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+x){y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+x){[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+x)(\\frac{8}{3}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+x\\,dx & =\\frac{8}{3}{[x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(3+\\frac{9}{2})-0 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278066 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278548 1069 "q-161519" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278548 1071 "![](images/91/91e8b39eb1bf181ceaf5b33b25fbb5ed0fa3ca7f7c1f0bb830c3337f142eb1dd.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278548 1072 #uuid "6f0f71b1-9aab-57d1-9125-17d6f5018a77" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278548 1074 "![](images/5c/5c8b476bceea5225cd230bd6e881706269b6eeda415503266e96e6e3c676b237.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31f2ff96afa1248427c1a6dd5074c47190c70983d7c0d5dfdd3def8bd883aef8.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+y}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+y}{z}^{2}\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{z}^{3}}{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=1+y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{(1+y)^{3}}{3}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{(1+y)^{3}}{3}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{(1+y)^{3}}{3}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{(1+y)^{4}}{12}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}(81-1)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{3}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{3}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{20}{3}x]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278042 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278549 1069 "q-161526" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278549 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=⟨\\sqrt{4-t},\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,(t-2)^{3}⟩.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278549 1072 #uuid "3678df18-cc7e-52b1-89db-fdff4ade3816" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278549 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{4-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,4].$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component ${t}^{2}-1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $(t-2)^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/af/af217bfbda3db04572cbfbb0b1aa8c18a6bb94c127c3d034dbfe3d96d6abbf80.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,4].$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278025 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278550 1069 "q-161528" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278550 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=⟨3{t}^{3}-1,\\,(t+1)^{2},\\,\\sqrt{t+9}⟩.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278550 1072 #uuid "8224800a-c120-51be-b3bd-74728426065c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278550 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $3{t}^{3}-1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $(t+1)^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{t+9}$ is $t\\in [-9,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c424889be78d14cca345aabd0c1f8289d7bba4c893a17feb6855540cad337fe.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in [-9,\\infty ).$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277780 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278551 1069 "q-161536" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278551 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278551 1072 #uuid "d858f67e-e87d-5922-848f-1cf10db83bc5" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278551 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x}} \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot ({e}^{x})-(x+{e}^{x})\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-y{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}{e}^{x}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{x}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277875 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278552 1069 "q-161539" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278552 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278552 1072 #uuid "d38df384-c529-5d23-b152-2f8b3a09208a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278552 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)} \\\\  & =⟨{{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(-y-x+1)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-xy+y]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277890 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278553 1069 "q-161541" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278553 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2\\sqrt{xy}$ for $3\\le x\\le 4$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278553 1072 #uuid "1788e522-3caf-5313-8725-17c2fd98bec0" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278553 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =⟨{{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{4xy+1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\,{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}+4xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{[-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x{y}^{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+8x+0-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278142 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278554 1069 "q-161542" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278554 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278554 1072 #uuid "caf172ee-9d85-5048-a736-0f63395a41ce" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278554 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot (2x)-(y-x)\\cdot (2y)+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2}+2xy+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[y-{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(1-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-(-1+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-(-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277854 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278555 1069 "q-161545" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278555 1071 "Consider the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,yz\\le x\\le 4},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $yz$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278555 1072 #uuid "ef487dd1-5505-5bf5-9562-86954dcf2c40" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278555 1074 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=yz\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{yz}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277748 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278556 1069 "q-161548" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278556 1071 "Consider the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,-3\\le x\\le 5{y}^{4}+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $yz$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278556 1072 #uuid "587a8773-2afe-542a-990e-6aafa5beb6ef" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278556 1074 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-3\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)=5{y}^{4}+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-3}^{5{y}^{4}+2}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278110 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278557 1069 "q-161552" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278557 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278557 1072 #uuid "d0720c99-cc1f-5800-96bf-793fcc7002e7" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278557 1074 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2xz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\ F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2y(2x+y),\\,2x(y+2x+y)⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨{{3{x}^{3}y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{4xy+2{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{4xy+4{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\,2yz,\\,2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(3{x}^{3}y)\\cdot (2)-(4xy+2{y}^{2})\\cdot (1)+4xy+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-12-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278092 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278558 1069 "q-161555" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278558 1071 "![](images/dc/dcc7c78752700a17970da69c3809a8c621aaf23580ab0c3329335083858bd019.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278558 1072 #uuid "2be6e8f8-0e9b-5231-8a0e-367e35f555ac" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278558 1074 "For a type II region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type II region enclosed between the back surface $x={u}_{1}(y,z)=1$ and the front surface $x={u}_{2}(y,z)=3.$\n\n![](images/33/339d4658909da42d55a836af93c7fda425bbe8d0ce17f246b73a4ca28cb9f380.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,1\\le x\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I *plane* region in the $yz$-plane with the upper curve\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n![](images/87/879425adc3b1e78a7ea6a59740c9d8634ac13f62dea8eedc764bb5f1b07c92c2.png)\n\nHence, $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}2y[x]_{x=1}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}4y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277889 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278559 1069 "q-161560" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278559 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+2z}2\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2y}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278559 1072 #uuid "5f527883-8338-55f7-8dc9-3b3113ec0c27" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278559 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+2z}2\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2[x]_{x=0}^{x=y+2z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(y+2z-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y+4z\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2y+4z\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}2y+4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[2yz+2{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2y(2y)+2(2y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}4{y}^{2}+8{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}12{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[4{y}^{3}]_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =4(27-1) \\\\  & =104\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278109 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278560 1069 "q-161561" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278560 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}4xz\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{4-2y}}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278560 1072 #uuid "a2982d5e-855b-5753-9807-1b56cd50c77d" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278560 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}4xz\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}[2z{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2z({z}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2y}}2{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{4-2y}}\\,\\text{d