4,\\,2y\\le z\\le 8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{2y}^{8}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dz\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{2y}^{8}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2y+8.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277825 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278562 1069 "q-161564" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278562 1071 "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nis defined over the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-z\\le y\\le 1,\\,4\\le z\\le 5,\\,-\\sqrt{y+z}\\le x\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278562 1072 #uuid "4ac74bdd-4f55-52a9-8415-18543bfb88ec" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278562 1074 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a9/a905e1f60fb0aa865c071fcbccbfecb30dcac7a74b4b647d24a40f4c89ff1935.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-\\sqrt{y+z},\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-z\\le y\\le 1,\\,4\\le z\\le 5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{-z}^{1}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dy\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{-z}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y+z}}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=4+5=9.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277859 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278563 1069 "q-161566" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278563 1071 "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nis defined over the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-8\\le y\\le {sin}^{2}⁡z,\\,0\\le z\\le \\pi ,\\,-2y-1\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+2z}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278563 1072 #uuid "29632302-5883-5021-9de6-855ebf0325c6" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278563 1074 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a9/a905e1f60fb0aa865c071fcbccbfecb30dcac7a74b4b647d24a40f4c89ff1935.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-2y-1,\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)={y}^{2}+2z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-8\\le y\\le {sin}^{2}⁡z,\\,0\\le z\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-8}^{{sin}^{2}⁡z}[{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dy\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-8}^{{sin}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{-2y-1}^{{y}^{2}+2z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=0+\\pi =\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278058 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278564 1069 "q-161569" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278564 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{4}xyz\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278564 1072 #uuid "1ad42c7c-d985-5ab2-9d5c-fd1f194eb10f" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278564 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{4}xyz\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}yz{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x={z}^{2}}^{x=4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}yz(\\frac{16}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8yz-\\frac{1}{2}y{z}^{5}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8yz-\\frac{1}{2}y{z}^{5}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8yz-\\frac{1}{2}y{z}^{5}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}4y[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=2}-\\frac{y}{12}[{z}^{6}]_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}4y(4-0)-\\frac{y}{12}(64-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}16y-\\frac{16}{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{32}{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{[{y}^{2}]}_{y=-1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}(4-1) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277745 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278565 1069 "q-161570" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278565 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}4x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le y}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278565 1072 #uuid "0286c07f-b1b2-5473-afd1-f9920e12c0a5" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278565 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}4x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({(\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2{y}^{2}z+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(81-0) \\\\  & =54\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277796 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278566 1069 "q-161571" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278566 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2z}}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,y\\le z\\le y+1}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278566 1072 #uuid "4d010482-1691-5f93-8b77-314a6b05233e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278566 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2z}}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{4}[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{2z}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{4}(2z-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{2}z\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{2}z\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{y+1}\\frac{1}{2}z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}[{z}^{2}]_{z=y}^{z=y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}((y+1)^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}(2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[{y}^{2}+y]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(9+3)-\\frac{1}{4}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277764 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278567 1069 "q-161573" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278567 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{y}^{z}(y+z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278567 1072 #uuid "5cb142b3-38e4-5365-8776-d017f7b45c07" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278567 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{y}^{z}(y+z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(y+z)[x]_{x=y}^{x=z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+z)(z-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{3}{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z]}_{z=y}^{z=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}(27-{y}^{3})-{y}^{2}(3-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}9-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}9-3{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[9y-{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =9-1+\\frac{1}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278015 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278568 1069 "q-161575" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278568 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2+2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,-z\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278568 1072 #uuid "cf24dc38-62d4-5f8d-aead-cd979e84a570" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278568 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2+2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-z}^{0}2+2{e}^{x}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-z}^{0}2+2{e}^{x}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-z}^{0}2+2{e}^{x}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x+2{e}^{x}]_{x=-z}^{x=0}dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}0+2{e}^{0}-2(-z)-2{e}^{-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+2z-2{e}^{-z}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+2z-2{e}^{-z}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+2z-2{e}^{-z}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[2z+{z}^{2}+2{e}^{-z}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2+1+2{e}^{-1})-2{e}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}1+2{e}^{-1}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}1+2{e}^{-1}\\,dy & =(1+2{e}^{-1})[y]_{y=-1}^{y=0} \\\\  & =(1+2{e}^{-1})(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277906 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278569 1069 "q-161579" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278569 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}18x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le yz}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278569 1072 #uuid "715930b9-ae45-5670-b2b8-93d918c8dca7" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278569 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}18x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{yz}18x\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{yz}18x\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{yz}18x\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[9{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=yz}dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}9(yz)^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}9{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}9{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}9{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[3{y}^{2}{z}^{3}]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,dy & =[{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277958 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278570 1069 "q-161581" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278570 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le y+z+2},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the $z$-axis and the line $z=2-y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278570 1072 #uuid "66331791-83b0-58ae-948f-5ef4e17dacab" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278570 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+z+2}y\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+z+2}y\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}y[x]_{x=0}^{x=y+z+2}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y(y+z+2)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+yz+2y\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b473cc75dbbd6fa36ba43f6bbdec4a0133164e6fd8bc5f76ff86cec74714a256.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+yz+2y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}{y}^{2}+yz+2y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}{y}^{2}+yz+2y\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[({y}^{2}+2y)z+\\frac{1}{2}y{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2-y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+2y)(2-y)+\\frac{1}{2}y(2-y)^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4y-{y}^{3})+\\frac{1}{2}y(4-4y+{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}+6y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =-2-\\frac{16}{3}+12 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277893 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278571 1069 "q-161583" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278571 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le yz},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the line $y=1$ and the parabola $z={y}^{2}$ in the first quadrant." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278571 1072 #uuid "40c7ad15-9d9f-5fe8-85c1-e6adcf3df389" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278571 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{yz}2x\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{yz}2x\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=yz}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(yz)^{2}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/acb173db9d28ed8b38e63c7bc365c20e5755aeb1af3f6340e7b9229a4b49101d.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}{z}^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z={y}^{2}}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}({y}^{6}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{8}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{9}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{9}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278174 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278572 1069 "q-161585" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278572 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the line $y=1$ and the parabola $z=-{y}^{2}$ in the fourth quadrant." 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278572 1072 #uuid "a816d812-5042-53f7-abc8-46b6718aff74" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278572 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1}dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1}dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}[x]_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/38/38ea49a6fae41574107b2ba68a578586a306cf43d419d4de5699dcfa009137c2.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{0}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}}^{0}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+1\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[({y}^{2}+1)z+\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=-{y}^{2}}^{z=0}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+1)((0-(-{y}^{2}))+\\frac{1}{3}(0-{(-{y}^{2})}^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+1){y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{6}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{6}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{21}{y}^{7}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{105}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278155 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278573 1069 "q-161589" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278573 1071 "![](images/d5/d5690bff5819d72cc9d49016c119dc79f62f4d766c142a43a89df196ce9f2335.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278573 1072 #uuid "5d288958-0f39-531c-abbd-563a70ab640a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278573 1074 "![](images/24/2427e053c0e2d808b32db5d7fedf81365e7e50f0b3f2dc61598ad1795394686e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2+2y-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/14/145f0d494ffe24de84e3a289b3a7316d3f4933f193d734aed118609f7fb9f94f.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2+2y-z}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2+2y-z}{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2+2y-z}{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2+2y-z}{y}^{2}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2+2y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(2+2y-z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}z\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}z\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}z\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{y}^{2}z+2{y}^{3}z-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278060 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278574 1069 "q-161590" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278574 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 16}.\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278574 1072 #uuid "f5a4373e-6e45-5815-aca4-6d44f34a430e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278574 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1074330fc650cf6af682f173f50943d779819afa59bf5be19edb88def1ef03a.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=16.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=16\\,}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277807 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278575 1069 "q-161594" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278575 1071 "![](images/f1/f1f3493cd1ff0bee115bf7c311504a2b8a9bbd38dd35adae96c9f7abcae40ae1.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V=$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278575 1072 #uuid "bbdba5ca-83e0-5a85-b98f-07e26739da4e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278575 1074 "For a type II region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type II region enclosed between the back surface $x={u}_{1}(y,z)=1$ and the front surface $x={u}_{2}(y,z)=2.$\n\n![](images/6c/6cc327f63d7141c43852e868812a7cff1ec9ae0acfed636ff896ceb204b6fbf0.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,1\\le x\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I *plane* region in the $yz$-plane with the upper curve\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a0fb19d32c2545e5c2a61cbb54536e7c28b1a54dbce51c7752c3687035d6c3d6.png)\n\nHence, $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}[{x}^{2}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277734 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278576 1069 "q-161599" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278576 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2y\\ge {x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278576 1072 #uuid "fba7584d-6a44-58f1-91c4-4ca141af635a" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278576 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0fc95ee69ce7121c0cf8ae68029b135d9667f50275227d141f1d979f85408859.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the  parabola $-2y={x}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2y={x}^{2}\\,}.\n$$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277736 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278577 1069 "q-161601" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278577 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278577 1072 #uuid "2307ae9e-a249-5090-bfed-df0660f7b0eb" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278577 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff1f735266f66879834e02ca7601e90e40dfa7929e5796d4a3f8cb4ff2828de7.png)\n\n  \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278047 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278578 1069 "q-161622" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278578 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1+x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278578 1072 #uuid "4104ec23-658c-58b7-8dbf-72086dab259b" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278578 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e7d8c11ac4b3de8473741a362ad2b78555c764da2d61f955435e8d59771cf46.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277768 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278579 1069 "q-161624" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278579 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278579 1072 #uuid "35b15061-fc8b-55f3-8742-4ca105208a91" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278579 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/8414de2e05da43c03fae3c8b1ff2102401e990188e9fd4ee59a24308ec34b086.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we have seen, $∂A$ consists of two concentric circles: the outer one is in $A,$ while the inner one is not.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains some of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277946 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278580 1069 "q-161625" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278580 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 2-x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278580 1072 #uuid "be0484aa-ac87-5330-9ede-cd54e1386a15" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278580 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cdca164d343d8bff4c78a1e366e52c588ca6c54dfdea61acea1900bcbdc79f31.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. As we have seen, $A$ contains all its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278028 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278581 1069 "q-161626" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278581 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∂A$\n- $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278581 1072 #uuid "baee2889-195d-5ff5-b660-0a9acac1604c" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278581 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/45/4540e03fc5404474a0cd900c89004e6a5cfe7ac2d4702348e9d0395fc1c914e9.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.  \n\n![](images/2a/2a93b912f0d7b3a84bf0ec9c3b3596d080a9e71a345ff436e2f591edf042d9db.png)\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ or, equivalently,  $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278159 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278582 1069 "q-161638" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278582 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<2-{x}^{2}}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278582 1072 #uuid "8fb05970-59dc-5904-9b90-0c1d735b4269" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278582 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277740 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278583 1069 "q-161640" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278583 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-3<x<-1,\\,1<y<0.5x+3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<2}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278583 1072 #uuid "20984d3c-4813-50f9-a16b-ba04fcaf3833" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278583 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277913 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278584 1069 "q-161641" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278584 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\frac{1}{x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\sqrt{1-x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 8,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278584 1072 #uuid "3ae695d6-ab71-5a57-95a1-94d938d5de7e" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278584 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277751 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278585 1069 "q-161642" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278585 1071 "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278585 1072 #uuid "168b395c-d069-5c8f-9acc-5bed4b37b963" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278585 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277977 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278586 1069 "q-161643" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278586 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278586 1072 #uuid "32d0249e-396e-5abf-9c3e-a9a01870f500" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278586 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277837 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278587 1069 "q-161646" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278587 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-3<x<3,\\,0<y<|2x|}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\,1<y<3-{x}^{2}}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278587 1072 #uuid "c3f47e10-181c-587e-a0be-2b1e263782c0" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278587 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278148 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278588 1069 "q-161647" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278588 1071 "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0,\\,0<y<\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-2<x<2,\\,0<y<{x}^{2}+1}$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278588 1072 #uuid "fdca40a5-5172-5279-8887-174acedd2d48" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278588 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278129 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278589 1069 "q-161651" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278589 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $A=∂A\\cup \\text{int}A$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278589 1072 #uuid "91207617-bfa9-5efa-a562-b7d640b0c541" 13194139533404 17592186045585 true] [17592186278589 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a39ecae41a88e873fa9eecb30a5b554be9ec98cf630851715ab35f2f873710f2.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and III are false. Since ${C}_{1}⊂∂A-A,$ we must have that $∂A⊄A$ and also that $A\\ne ∂A\\cup \\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except 